\section{Optimum contribution selection}

The predicted genetic values can also be used to choose the proportion
of genes contributed by each parent to the next generation. For an
additive model, the expected genetic value of the resulting population
is the contribution-weighted mean of these parental values.
\cde{st.ocs} maximizes this mean subject to an upper bound on group
relatedness and lower and upper bounds on each contribution
(Wellmann, 2019). These continuous proportions sum to one.

The following analysis uses the same tropical-maize lines and fitted
marker effects as the cross evaluations. Predicted genetic values supply
the named merit vector. The genomic relationship matrix, \cde{A}, is
centered using marker frequencies in this complete candidate set. The
matrix and its reference remain fixed when contributions change.
Here \cde{K=A/2} is the kinship-scaled matrix, and group relatedness is
the quadratic form of \cde{K} with the contribution vector. In this
example it is a genomic relatedness index relative to the candidate
panel, rather than an absolute probability of identity by descent.
Equal contributions to all candidates have an index approximately zero
on this centered reference.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
values <- gs.estimate.gv(estimation.set="effects01",
                         prediction.set="effects01")
merit <- setNames(values$yhat, values$i)
A <- st.get.A("effects01")
merit <- merit[rownames(A)]
K <- A/2
@

The chosen breeding scenario sets \cde{theta=0.02} as the bound on group
relatedness on this reference scale. Each contribution
has a lower bound of zero and an upper bound of 0.1, so a single parent
can supply at most ten percent of the nominal gene contribution.
The merit vector is ordered explicitly to match both dimensions of \cde{K}.
The returned \cde{gain} is the contribution-weighted mean of the supplied
merit values.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
contributions <- st.ocs(
    merit = merit,                             # Parental genetic values
    K = K,                                     # Kinship matrix
    theta = 0.02,                              # Group relatedness limit
    lower = 0,                                 # Minimum parental contribution
    upper = 0.1                                # Maximum parental contribution
)
contributions
@
\begin{Verbatim}[frame=single,fontsize=\footnotesize,formatcom=\color{bl}]
Optimum contribution selection
Criterion: maximum expected genetic merit
Status: optimal
Expected merit: 2.7698
Group relatedness: 0.0200; requested limit: 0.0200
