  [221 table rows omitted]
  258           0.0000
  259           0.0000
  261           0.0000
  263           0.0000
  264           0.0000
\end{Verbatim}

\subsubsection*{Interpretation of the printed summary}

The summary above the contribution table reports the objective and the
numerical qualification of the solution.
\begin{description}
\item[Criterion and status.] The criterion is the quantity maximized.
\cde{optimal} denotes a certified merit optimum; \cde{maxit} denotes an
unresolved iteration or numerical limit. An infeasible request has no
solution under the supplied constraints.
\item[Expected merit.] This is the contribution-weighted mean of the
supplied parental genetic values.
\item[Group relatedness.] This is the achieved $c'Kc$, compared with the
requested limit \cde{theta}.
\item[Minimum relatedness and lower bound.] These describe the separate
search for the smallest attainable relatedness under the contribution
bounds. Their difference measures its remaining numerical gap.
\item[Gain upper bound and optimality gap.] The bound limits the best
attainable merit. Its difference from the achieved merit is the gap.
\item[Maximum constraint residual.] This is the largest numerical
violation of the constraints; values close to zero indicate feasibility
within numerical tolerance.
\end{description}
The contribution table identifies the parents and their gene shares, which
sum to one. Its displayed rows include the greatest and smallest shares.

The result also reports the minimum-relatedness calculation for the supplied
contribution bounds. A requested \cde{theta} below the feasible range is
infeasible. The returned status and diagnostics describe feasibility and
numerical convergence.
These contributions refer to the nominal gene shares before selection
within progeny families. A crossing plan additionally specifies parental
pairs and integer progeny counts; realized ancestry after selection can
differ from the nominal contributions.


\section{Optimum cross selection}

\cde{st.cross.plan} selects parent pairs for a specified number of families.
The default joint mode chooses both parental usage and pairing to
maximize the predicted additive population mean,
subject to contribution limits and the group relatedness bound. The
specified number of crosses refers to distinct unordered pairs of different
parents. Here every family has the same progeny count.

\subsection{A complete observed-marker panel}

The execution example uses a separate panel comprising the first 30 lines
in the processed data order. Markers with any missing call among these
parents are excluded, leaving 243 markers with complete homozygous founder
genotypes. The original intercept and the fitted effects of these retained
markers define the prediction model for this example. Merit and the genomic
relationship matrix are recalculated for precisely this panel. Its marker
set and reference frequencies differ from the preceding 236-line analysis;
the relatedness bound therefore has this smaller panel as its reference.
Planning and simulation below share the same parents, markers and effect model.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
marker.effects <- gs.return.effects(data.set="effects01")
st.copy.marker.data("cross.parents", "effects01")
st.restrict.marker.data(ind.list=names(merit)[1:30],
                        data.set="cross.parents")
st.restrict.marker.data(MaMis.MAX=0, data.set="cross.parents")
cross.map <- st.get.map(data.set="cross.parents")
@

Marker-data copies do not retain fitted effects. The marker names from the
map identify the original effects attached by \cde{gs.set.effects}, together
with the fitted intercept.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
cross.effects <- marker.effects[
  marker.effects$marker %in% c("mu", cross.map$Name), ]
gs.set.effects(cross.effects, data.set="cross.parents")
cross.values <- gs.estimate.gv(estimation.set="cross.parents",
                               prediction.set="cross.parents")
@

The named merit vector follows the parent order of \cde{cross.A}.
Dividing this additive relationship matrix by two supplies the kinship
scale used in the following plans.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
cross.merit <- setNames(cross.values$yhat, cross.values$i)
cross.A <- st.get.A("cross.parents")
cross.merit <- cross.merit[rownames(cross.A)]
cross.K <- cross.A/2
@

\subsection{Joint selection of parents and crosses by mean}

The following breeding scenario contains 15 distinct families with 100
progeny each. A parental contribution of at most 0.1 permits a parent to
occur in at most three of these equal-sized families. Group relatedness
is bounded by \cde{theta=0.02} on the 30-parent reference scale.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
cross.plan <- st.cross.plan(
    merit = cross.merit,                       # Parental genetic values
    K = cross.K,                               # Kinship matrix
    n.crosses = 15,                            # Number of distinct parent pairs
    n.progeny = 100,                           # Progeny per family
    theta = 0.02,                              # Group relatedness limit
    upper = 0.1                                # Maximum parental contribution
)
cross.plan
@
\begin{Verbatim}[frame=single,fontsize=\footnotesize,formatcom=\color{bl}]
