\subsection{Simulation of the planned DH families}
\label{sec:planned-dh}

The simulation uses complete homozygous founders. \cde{st.set.simpop}
initializes them from the marker data set. The parental names in the plan
identify the individuals copied into the two temporary parental populations.
\cde{cross} creates one F1 from each pair, and \cde{dh} produces the planned
number of doubled-haploid descendants from that F1. The seed is set before
the first cross.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
st.set.simpop(pop.name="Founders", data.set="cross.parents")
rng.init(seed=1001)
founder.names <- population.pedigree("Founders")$name
planned.crosses <- cross.plan$crosses

for (i in seq_len(nrow(planned.crosses))) {
    population.copy("P1", "Founders",
                    start=match(planned.crosses$P1Name[i], founder.names),
                    n=1)
    population.copy("P2", "Founders",
                    start=match(planned.crosses$P2Name[i], founder.names),
                    n=1)
    cross("F1", "P1", "P2", NoPg=1, crossing.scheme=3)
    dh(sprintf("DH%03d", i), "F1",
       NoPg=planned.crosses$n.progeny[i])
}
population.remove(c("P1", "P2", "F1"))
families <- population.list()
families
@
\begin{Verbatim}[frame=single,fontsize=\footnotesize,formatcom=\color{bl}]
    PopName count
1     DH015   100
2     DH014   100
3     DH013   100
4     DH012   100
5     DH011   100
6     DH010   100
7     DH009   100
8     DH008   100
9     DH007   100
10    DH006   100
11    DH005   100
12    DH004   100
13    DH003   100
14    DH002   100
15    DH001   100
16 Founders    30
\end{Verbatim}

\cde{population.list} reports the simulation populations and their sizes.
The temporary parental and F1 populations have been removed. The 15 DH
families contain 100 individuals each, giving 1,500 simulated DH individuals;
\cde{Founders} retains the 30 parental lines.

\cde{population.concat} moves the individuals from each DH family into
one population named \cde{DH}. The separate family populations are removed,
while their individuals and pedigrees are retained in the merged population.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
for (i in seq_len(nrow(planned.crosses))) {
    population.concat("DH", sprintf("DH%03d", i))
}
merged.populations <- population.list()
merged.populations
@
\begin{Verbatim}[frame=single,fontsize=\footnotesize,formatcom=\color{bl}]
   PopName count
1       DH  1500
2 Founders    30
\end{Verbatim}

The simulation uses the fitted marker effects and intercept transferred
by \cde{st.set.simpop}. Genotyping and evaluation provide genetic values
for the founders and the merged DH population under this same model.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
genotype.population("Founders DH")
evaluate.population("Founders DH", effName="effect")
genetic.summary <- summarize.gvalue("Founders DH", effectfile="effect")
genetic.summary
@
\begin{Verbatim}[frame=single,fontsize=\footnotesize,formatcom=\color{bl}]
          Obs     Mean      SDev      Min      Q10      Med      Max
Founders   30 2.187315 0.2997672 1.653617 1.799855 2.210782 2.805240
DH       1500 2.383872 0.2514697 1.650580 2.040985 2.383192 3.035688
\end{Verbatim}

The selection gain is the difference between the mean of the simulated
DH population and the equal-weight mean of the 30 founder lines. It
reflects the choice of crossing parents before selection among the DH
individuals, under the fitted additive model.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
selection.gain <- genetic.summary["DH", "Mean"] -
                  genetic.summary["Founders", "Mean"]
selection.gain
@
\begin{Verbatim}[frame=single,fontsize=\footnotesize,formatcom=\color{bl}]
[1] 0.1965574
\end{Verbatim}

The plan predicts the mean of the unselected DH population. Its predicted
selection gain uses the same founder mean as the simulated gain. The
comparison therefore concerns the same genetic model and founder reference.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
prediction.comparison <- data.frame(
    Predicted = c(cross.plan$gain,
                  cross.plan$gain - genetic.summary["Founders", "Mean"]),
    Simulated = c(genetic.summary["DH", "Mean"], selection.gain),
    row.names = c("Population mean", "Selection gain")
)
prediction.comparison$Difference <- prediction.comparison$Simulated -
                                    prediction.comparison$Predicted
prediction.comparison
@
\begin{Verbatim}[frame=single,fontsize=\footnotesize,formatcom=\color{bl}]
                Predicted Simulated   Difference
Population mean 2.3853515 2.3838720 -0.001479536
Selection gain  0.1980369 0.1965574 -0.001479536
\end{Verbatim}

The difference is the simulated value minus the prediction. Finite
Mendelian sampling can make the simulated population mean differ from its
expectation. This comparison precedes selection among DH lines; the
usefulness criterion describes a different, within-family selection scenario.

\section{Optimum cross selection using usefulness}

The preceding mean criterion can be compared with usefulness while retaining
the same 15 families, 100 progeny per family, candidate pairs, parental-use
limits and group-relatedness bound. In this example, ten descendants are
retained from each family. Equal retained counts give each family the same
weight in the predicted mean of the selected descendants.

\subsection{One score table for both criteria}

\cde{gs.cross.eval.mu} and \cde{gs.cross.eval.va} evaluate the means and
DH segregation variances for the complete founder panel and its existing
effect model. The \cde{N=10} and \cde{G=100} arguments specify ten retained
individuals among 100 progeny. The resulting \cde{es} score uses the
finite-family approximation of Burrows (1972); it is not an exact
expectation over finite normal order statistics.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
gs.cross.eval.mu(data.set="cross.parents")
gs.cross.eval.va(pop.type="DH", data.set="cross.parents")
gs.cross.eval.es(N=10, G=100, data.set="cross.parents")
cross.scores <- gs.cross.info(data.set="cross.parents")
@

Both analyses receive the same values from \cde{cross.scores}.
The mean criterion maximizes the average \cde{mu} of the selected crosses.
The result also reports their average usefulness.
Both searches receive the same earlier feasible \cde{cross.plan} through
\cde{control}; each can retain or improve upon that starting crossing list.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
mean.plan <- st.cross.plan(
    merit = cross.merit,                       # Parental genetic values
    K = cross.K,                               # Kinship matrix
    n.crosses = 15,                            # Number of distinct parent pairs
    n.progeny = 100,                           # Progeny per family
    theta = 0.02,                              # Group relatedness limit
    upper = 0.1,                               # Maximum parental contribution
    scores = cross.scores,                     # Precomputed cross scores
    criterion = "mean",                        # Objective: maximum cross mean
    control = list(                            # Search settings
        start = cross.plan                     # Starting crossing plan
    )
)
mean.plan
@
