  1         4             0.0016  2.1884
  1         5             0.0063  2.2508
  1         6             0.0088  2.4093
  1         7             0.0048  2.3017
  1         11            0.0148  2.4787
  1         14            0.0367  2.5445
  1         16            0.0036  2.1252
  1         21            0.0032  2.2774
  [420 table rows omitted]
  38        40            0.0003  2.1747
  38        41            0.0023  2.4224
  39        40            0.0002  1.7876
  39        41            0.0006  2.0352
  40        41            0.0003  2.0211
\end{Verbatim}

\subsubsection*{Interpretation of the printed summary}

\begin{description}
\item[Status.] \cde{optimal} means that the constraint residual and
merit-bound gap satisfy the numerical tolerance. \cde{maxit} denotes an
unresolved iteration or numerical limit; \cde{infeasible} denotes a request
with no feasible allocation.
\item[Expected merit.] This is the allocation-weighted mean of the
supplied mating scores: cross means in this analysis and usefulness in
the following analysis.
\item[Reference F0 and F1.] \cde{F0} is the fixed reference value. Here
it is the mean of the complete kinship matrix. \cde{F1} is the
allocation-weighted kinship between the parents of the allowed matings.
\item[Group F2 and upper limit.] \cde{F2} is $c'Kc$ for the parental
gene contributions implied by the mating fractions. Its upper limit
follows from the supplied \cde{dF} values and reference.
\item[dF1 and dF2.] These express changes relative to \cde{F0} as
\[
dF_1=\frac{F_1-F_0}{1-F_0},\qquad
dF_2=1-\sqrt{\frac{1-F_2}{1-F_0}}.
\]
The first follows the supplied change interval, and the second is bounded
by its upper endpoint.
\item[Merit upper bound and gap.] The upper bound limits the best
attainable merit. The gap is its difference from the achieved merit.
\item[Maximum constraint residual.] This reports the largest numerical
constraint violation used to assess feasibility.
\end{description}
The allocation column gives fractions of the produced progeny, summing
to one. For example, 0.0107 corresponds to approximately 1.07\% of the
progeny. The merit column contains each mating's supplied score. These
continuous fractions are not integer family sizes.


Parental gene contributions are one half of the sum of mating allocations
across their two parental slots. The same parent in both slots would supply
the full contribution of that self mating; this candidate table contains
only distinct parents.

Different mating fractions can give the same parental contributions and
the same additive mean. The mean-based optimum can therefore have several
equally good mating allocations.

\subsection{Usefulness-based mate allocation}

The second mating table retains every pair and its bounds. Only the merit
column changes to the existing expected superior-progeny score. That score
was calculated above for ten retained individuals among 100 produced DH
progeny per family, using the same fitted effects and finite-family
approximation. The method optimizes the fixed supplied scores; it does not
recalculate the selection intensity from the allocation fractions.
A later integer allocation with different family sizes would require
scores appropriate to those sizes. The contribution constraints refer
to nominal gene shares before selection within families.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
oma.usefulness.matings <- oma.mean.matings
oma.usefulness.matings$merit <- cross.scores$es
@

The second call uses the same parent and mating bounds, kinship matrix,
reference and \cde{dF} interval as the mean analysis. Its weighted merit
therefore represents expected usefulness under that declared scoring model.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
oma.usefulness <- st.oma(
    parents = oma.parents,                     # Parent contribution bounds
    matings = oma.usefulness.matings,          # Allowed crosses and scores
    K = cross.K,                               # Kinship matrix
    dF = c(0, 0.01),                           # F1 change range; F2 change cap
    base = "RM"                                # Random-mating reference
)
oma.usefulness
@
