The diversity line reports the computed loss and its strict upper limit.
Feasibility requires the displayed estimate before rounding to be below
0.025. The reported search status retains its usual meaning: a feasible
heuristic result does not establish a global optimum. The constraint applies
to the declared parental sample and the Gamma estimate, rather than a
guaranteed loss of genetic variation in a future selected population.

\section{Allocation of progeny to fixed crosses}
\label{sec:progeny-allocation}

The parent pairs can remain fixed while the produced progeny counts vary.
The following allocation distributes 1,500 progeny across the 15 families
and retains ten individuals per family. The mean of the selected descendants
therefore weights each family by its ten retained individuals. Selection
intensity is recalculated for every proposed integer family size from the
cross mean and segregation variance; an earlier score at 100 progeny is not
reused at other family sizes.
The default selection differential is the finite-family approximation of
Burrows (1972).

<<eval=FALSE>>=
progeny.allocation <- st.cross.allocate(
    plan = diversity.plan,                     # Fixed parent pairs and constraints
    scores = cross.scores,                     # Cross means and segregation variances
    total.progeny = 1500,                      # Total produced progeny budget
    n.selected = 10,                           # Retained descendants per family
    min.progeny = 10,                          # Minimum produced count per family
    max.progeny = 1500,                        # Maximum produced count per family
    accuracy = 1                               # Within-family ranking accuracy
)
progeny.allocation
@
\begin{Verbatim}[frame=single,fontsize=\footnotesize,formatcom=\color{bl}]
Fixed-pair progeny allocation
Status: optimal
Families: 15; produced: 1500; retained: 150
Predicted retained mean: 2.652; response: 0.2816
Objective upper bound: 2.652; response gap: 6.661e-16
Group relatedness, nominal retained: 0.01982; produced: 0.02027
Family: parents; produced / retained; predicted retained mean
1: 1 x 32; 106 / 10; 2.595
2: 1 x 41; 94 / 10; 2.534
3: 2 x 5; 106 / 10; 2.608
4: 2 x 22; 83 / 10; 2.734
5: 3 x 37; 98 / 10; 2.23
6: 5 x 14; 96 / 10; 2.777
7: 6 x 11; 85 / 10; 2.824
8: 7 x 34; 113 / 10; 2.728
9: 11 x 33; 94 / 10; 2.693
10: 14 x 35; 102 / 10; 2.694
11: 14 x 40; 126 / 10; 2.681
12: 16 x 38; 100 / 10; 2.551
13: 21 x 22; 99 / 10; 2.743
14: 27 x 34; 94 / 10; 2.696
15: 29 x 38; 104 / 10; 2.688
\end{Verbatim}

The family table gives produced and retained counts together with the
recalculated expected selected-family means. The expected selected mean
combines them using retained counts. The budget and integer bounds refer
to produced counts. The optimum and numerical gap refer to allocation
among these fixed pairs under the finite-family approximation; they do not
establish a joint optimum over parent pairs and progeny numbers.

The inherited parental contribution and group-relatedness constraints use
nominal retained-family gene shares. Produced-progeny shares and their group
relatedness are separate reported quantities. Neither vector predicts the
actual genomic ancestry after within-family selection. The family-slot
diversity sample remains unchanged when only produced counts vary.

The low-level simulation commands in Section~\ref{sec:planned-dh} already
read the produced count of each family from \cde{planned.crosses}. The
assignment \cde{planned.crosses <- progeny.allocation\$crosses} therefore
provides the variable counts to that same crossing and DH-generation loop.
The equal-family execution adapter retains its equal-size requirement.

An alternative calculation uses finite-sample normal order statistics
following Okoye et al.\ (2026, Equations~4--8). The same fixed families,
retained counts and total budget are used below. The selection method
changes the expected selection differential; it does not change the fitted
cross means or segregation variances. The automatic allocator compares
the predicted gain from one additional individual in each family.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
progeny.order.allocation <- st.cross.allocate(
    plan = diversity.plan,                     # Fixed parent pairs and constraints
    scores = cross.scores,                     # Cross means and segregation variances
    total.progeny = 1500,                      # Total produced progeny budget
    n.selected = 10,                           # Retained descendants per family
    min.progeny = 10,                          # Minimum produced count per family
    max.progeny = 1500,                        # Maximum produced count per family
    accuracy = 1,                              # Within-family ranking accuracy
    selection.method = "order.statistics",     # Finite-normal selection expectation
    control = list(                            # Allocation settings
        algorithm = "auto"                     # Automatic discrete allocator
    )
)
progeny.order.allocation
@
\begin{Verbatim}[frame=single,fontsize=\footnotesize,formatcom=\color{bl}]
