The selection-method and algorithm lines identify the scored objective
and the allocator used. Produced counts again sum to 1,500 and each family
retains ten individuals. All 15 produced counts equal those of the
Burrows-based allocation above. The predicted retained mean now uses the
expected mean of the ten greatest values in a finite normal sample.
The status \cde{numerically.optimal} means that the estimated numerical gap
meets the requested tolerance. Quadrature error estimates are not rigorous
interval bounds. The mathematical integer optimum is conditional on fixed
pairs, fixed retained counts and the stated normal model; it does not
establish biological accuracy or a joint optimum over pairs and counts.

A common population evaluation summarizes the same fixed family counts.
The saved selection method and prediction accuracies are inherited from
the allocation. The kinship reference remains the named matrix used in
planning, and the inherited constraints are checked again from the supplied
counts and parents. G1 denotes the selected DH descendants of the current
crosses. G2 would follow an additional cycle of crossing selected DH lines,
DH derivation and selection.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
cross.population <- st.cross.evaluate(
    plan = progeny.order.allocation,           # Fixed families and produced counts
    K = cross.K                                # Original named kinship reference
)
cross.population
@
