\end{Verbatim}

The status \cde{evaluated} identifies completion of this population
calculation. The produced marginal variance, 0.04812, describes the genetic value of a
randomly sampled candidate from the mixture of families. It combines the
within-family term 0.02753 and the between-family term 0.02060. The selected
G1 mean, approximately 2.652, instead weights each family by its retained
count. Its response, 0.2817, uses the unselected family means on these same
retained-count weights as the baseline, approximately 2.370.

The family, produced and retained contribution bases describe nominal
parental shares. Equal retained counts make the family and retained
relatedness equal here, approximately 0.01982, while the produced-count
weights give 0.02027. These values use the original kinship matrix.
Within-family selection can alter genomic ancestry even when the retained
family counts remain fixed. The selected between-family term is 0.01875,
but selected within-family and total variance require additional moment
calculations. The genetic value and segregation variance after crossing
the selected DH lines for a
further breeding cycle remain unavailable. Evaluation checks the supplied
population; it does not optimize the plan or alter its counts.

\subsection{Comparison with simulated selected descendants}

A separate repeated-simulation check uses the same 30 founders, 243 markers,
fitted effects and fixed pairs as \cde{progeny.allocation}. The table retains
the Burrows-based predictions and allocation above, with equal families as
the comparison. Each of 200 paired cycles
produces 1,500 DH descendants under each allocation and retains the ten
highest genetic values per family. Each paired cycle uses the same seed
under both allocations, with seeds 740001 through 740200. The table reports
the predicted selected mean, simulated mean across cycles, and Monte Carlo
standard error (MCSE).
The difference uses the paired cycles.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
allocation.validation
@
\begin{Verbatim}[frame=single,fontsize=\footnotesize,formatcom=\color{bl}]
               Quantity   Predicted    Simulated         MCSE
1        Equal families 2.651319231 2.6477827455 0.0005485178
2    Allocated families 2.651797776 2.6483499520 0.0005351892
3 Allocation difference 0.000478545 0.0005672066 0.0003372739
\end{Verbatim}

The simulated allocation difference is positive. Its approximate 95\%
Monte Carlo interval runs from $-0.000094$ to $0.001228$ and includes zero.
This run therefore does not establish a positive allocation benefit at
that precision.
The equal-count and allocated simulated means are approximately 0.00354
and 0.00345 below their Burrows predictions, respectively. That discrepancy
concerns the selection approximation for this
fitted-effect model, rather than a failure of the integer allocation optimum.
Re-evaluation of these same equal and allocated counts with finite-normal
order statistics increases their predicted means by approximately
$0.0000317340$ and $0.0000317367$, respectively. These changes are much
smaller than the discrepancy
from simulation, whose explanation therefore remains open.
The same marker effects score the predictions and simulations, so the
comparison assesses model consistency and does not provide independent field
validation. These repeated simulations are validation work, separate from
the ordinary short companion example.


\section{Reproductive roles and parent capacities}
\label{sec:practical-constraints}

The same maize markers and fitted effects also support a smaller example
with the first six parents of the crossing panel. Marker-data-set copying
and restriction keep the scores specific to those six candidates. The
kinship submatrix retains the scale of the original 30-parent reference;
its allele-frequency reference is not re-estimated after subsetting.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
practical.ids <- names(cross.merit)[1:6]
practical.K <- cross.K[practical.ids, practical.ids]
st.copy.marker.data(
    "practical.parents",                       # Six-parent copy for this policy
    "cross.parents"                            # Existing real marker panel
)
st.restrict.marker.data(
    ind.list = practical.ids,                  # Six parents from the real panel
    data.set = "practical.parents"             # Six-parent marker data set
)
gs.set.effects(
    cross.effects,                             # Fitted effects for the same markers
    data.set = "practical.parents"             # Six-parent marker data set
)
gs.cross.eval.mu(
    data.set = "practical.parents"             # Recomputed cross means
)
gs.cross.eval.va(
    pop.type = "DH",                           # Segregation among derived DH lines
    data.set = "practical.parents"             # Six-parent marker data set
)
gs.cross.eval.es(
    N = 2,                                     # Retained individuals per family
    G = 20,                                    # Produced individuals per family
    data.set = "practical.parents"             # Six-parent marker data set
)
practical.scores <- gs.cross.info(
    data.set = "practical.parents"             # Scores for exactly the six parents
)
@

The first three candidates form an operational seed-parent pool, and the
other three form an operational pollen-parent pool. These imposed crossing
roles are management choices for this example, rather than biological sex
categories. The requested plan uses exactly five parents: two seed parents
and three pollen parents. Equal family use applies to the selected seed
parents, without requiring all eligible seed parents to be selected.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
practical.parents <- data.frame(
    parent = practical.ids,                    # Actual parent identifiers
    pool = rep(c("seed", "pollen"), each=3),   # Operational parent groups
    seed = rep(c(TRUE, FALSE), each=3),        # First group eligible as seed parent
    pollen = rep(c(FALSE, TRUE), each=3)       # Second group eligible as pollen parent
)
practical.limits <- data.frame(
    parent = NA_character_,                    # Group-level parent counts
    pool = c(NA, "seed", "pollen"),            # All parents or the named group
    role = c("all", "seed", "pollen"),         # Count parents in the stated role
    basis = "family.slots",                    # Distinct-family participation
    measure = "parents",                       # Number of distinct used parents
    lower = c(5, 2, 3),                        # Exact total, seed and pollen counts
    upper = c(5, 2, 3)                         # Equal lower and upper bounds
)
practical.equal.use <- data.frame(
    pool = "seed",                             # Seed-parent group
    role = "seed",                             # Seed use only
    basis = "family.slots"                     # Equal integer family use
)
@

The following search considers four distinct families with twenty produced
descendants each. The existing usefulness scores correspond to retaining
two descendants per family. The relatedness ceiling is nonrestrictive here,
so the example emphasizes the explicitly declared practical restrictions.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
practical.plan <- st.cross.plan(
    merit = cross.merit[practical.ids],        # Merits of the six eligible parents
    K = practical.K,                           # Original-scale kinship submatrix
    n.crosses = 4,                             # Four distinct biological families
    n.progeny = 20,                            # Equal produced count at this stage
    theta = max(diag(practical.K)),            # Nonrestrictive relatedness ceiling
    scores = practical.scores,                 # Scores for the six-parent panel
    criterion = "usefulness",                  # Expected selected-family performance
    constraints = list(                        # Intersecting practical restrictions
        parents = practical.parents,           # Pool labels and role eligibility
        limits = practical.limits,             # Exact used-parent counts
        equal.use = practical.equal.use        # Equal use among selected seed parents
    ),
    control = list(                            # Search settings
        method = "exact"                       # Bounded exhaustive cross search
    )
)
practical.plan
@

\begin{Verbatim}[frame=single,fontsize=\footnotesize,formatcom=\color{bl}]
