Maximum constraint residual: 0.0000

Chosen crosses
  Family  Parent 1  Parent 2  Progeny    Mean  Kinship  Usefulness
       1  1         5              20  2.2508  -0.0689      2.4556
       2  1         6              20  2.4093  -0.1318      2.6332
       3  2         4              20  2.2375   0.0140      2.5376
       4  2         6              20  2.4583  -0.0311      2.6710
\end{Verbatim}

All ten practical checks pass. The selected seed parents 1 and 2 each
occur in two families, and the pollen parents 4, 5 and 6 cover the four
families. The exact five-parent total includes two seed and three pollen
parents; parent 3 remains eligible but unused. The saved direction map
identifies reproductive roles independently of the canonical order of the
unordered biological parent pair. Status \cde{optimal} qualifies the
completed exact search for this declared model and its supplied scores.

The next allocation adds a produced-capacity limit of thirty descendant
slots for each pollen parent. Produced counts remain variable while the
four chosen families, reproductive directions and two retained individuals
per family remain fixed. A produced slot counts one descendant receiving
that parent's pollen contribution; it does not count laboratory crossing
operations.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
practical.capacity <- data.frame(
    parent = practical.ids[4:6],               # Individual pollen parents
    pool = NA_character_,                      # Individual selectors need no pool filter
    role = "pollen",                           # Pollen contribution only
    basis = "produced",                        # Produced descendant slots
    measure = "uses",                          # Integer incidences before division
    lower = 0,                                 # No minimum produced pollen use
    upper = 30                                 # At most thirty slots per pollen parent
)
practical.allocation <- st.cross.allocate(
    plan = practical.plan,                     # Fixed families and declared directions
    scores = practical.scores,                 # Cross means and segregation variances
    total.progeny = 80,                        # Total produced descendant budget
    n.selected = 2,                            # Retained descendants per family
    min.progeny = 2,                           # Minimum produced count per family
    max.progeny = 40,                          # Maximum produced count per family
    accuracy = 1,                              # Within-family ranking accuracy
    selection.method = "order.statistics",     # Finite-normal selection expectation
    constraints = list(                        # Additional produced-capacity restriction
        limits = practical.capacity            # Individual pollen-parent capacities
    )
)
practical.allocation
@

\begin{Verbatim}[frame=single,fontsize=\footnotesize,formatcom=\color{bl}]
Fixed-pair progeny allocation
Selection: finite normal order statistics; allocator:
constrained.enumeration
Status: numerically.optimal
Families: 4; produced: 80; retained: 8
Predicted retained mean: 2.574; response: 0.2352
Estimated objective upper bound: 2.574; response gap: 4.408e-14
Group relatedness, nominal retained: 0.1785; produced: 0.1744
Practical checks: feasible; pass 13, fail 0, unresolved 0, unavailable 0
Family: parents; produced / retained; predicted retained mean
1: 1 x 5; 21 / 2; 2.459
2: 1 x 6; 15 / 2; 2.614
3: 2 x 4; 29 / 2; 2.571
4: 2 x 6; 15 / 2; 2.653
\end{Verbatim}

The produced family counts 21, 15, 29 and 15 sum to eighty; two individuals
per family give eight retained descendants. Pollen parent 6 contributes
to the second and fourth families, using $15+15=30$ produced slots and
exactly reaching its capacity. Pollen parents 4 and 5 use 29 and 21 slots,
respectively. All thirteen practical checks pass.

With twenty descendants per family, pollen parent 6 would have forty
produced uses, exceeding the new capacity. The earlier plan with equal
family sizes therefore has a different feasible set; a difference in its
predicted selected mean is not a gain comparison against a feasible
baseline under the new restriction.

This produced-capacity constraint couples family sizes. The reported
allocator is \cde{constrained.\allowbreak enumeration}; the independent family-increment
theorem in the appendix does not certify this constrained allocation.
Status \cde{numerically.optimal} uses the estimated objective bound, with
response gap approximately $4.4\times10^{-14}$ here. The qualification
applies to the stated fixed-pair problem. Parent-count and equal-family-use
restrictions inherited from the crossing plan remain in force alongside
the new produced-capacity rows.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
practical.population <- st.cross.evaluate(
    plan = practical.allocation                # Independent inherited-constraint check
)
practical.population
