The evaluation recomputes all thirteen inherited and additional practical
checks from the actual family counts and declared directions; all pass.
This feasibility result is separate from the optimizer's source status.
The G1 retained mean is approximately 2.574, with response 0.2352 above
the retained-weight baseline of approximately 2.339.

The produced marginal variance is 0.03186. The retained between-family
term, 0.005243, is not a retained total variance; that total remains
unavailable. Nominal role, family, produced and retained shares retain
their declared denominators. They do not predict genomic ancestry after
selection; selected genomic diversity and the additional G2 breeding-cycle
forecast remain unavailable.

\section{Joint choice of crosses and family sizes}
\label{sec:joint-progeny}

The preceding allocation changes produced counts for a fixed set of
families. The \cde{progeny} argument of \cde{st.cross.plan} allows the
chosen biological families and their produced counts to vary together.
The retained count remains four per active family here. Selection acts
within each family, so all three families contribute equally to the
selected G1 mean even when their produced counts differ.

This comparison uses the same six maize founders and their fifteen
unordered candidate pairs, with ninety produced DH descendants and
twelve retained descendants. Every calculation uses the same cross means,
segregation variances, finite-normal selection expectation, accuracy and
original kinship reference. The seed and pollen policy from
Section~\ref{sec:practical-constraints} is not imposed in this separate
example. Thus the candidate graph is identical across the three designs.
The stored \cde{es} column was computed for another family size; the new
\cde{progeny} branch recomputes selection response from \cde{mu},
\cde{va} and the actual counts instead of using that column.

The equal-family baseline chooses three pairs with thirty produced
descendants each. The fixed-pair calculation then redistributes the same
ninety produced descendants over those pairs, within bounds four to fifty.
The joint calculation allows the pairs and counts to vary under those
same bounds. The equal-family design is feasible for both broader
problems, and the fixed-pair allocation is supplied as a checked starting
incumbent for the joint search. The production bounds declared by the
standalone allocator replace the source design's equal-size bounds;
inherited parental, pairing and practical restrictions still apply.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
joint.equal.time <- system.time({
    joint.equal <- st.cross.plan(
        merit = cross.merit[practical.ids],    # Merits of the same six founders
        K = practical.K,                       # Original-scale named kinship reference
        n.crosses = 3,                         # Three distinct biological families
        theta = max(diag(practical.K)),        # Nonrestrictive family-slot ceiling
        scores = practical.scores,             # Means and variances for all fifteen pairs
        criterion = "usefulness",              # Expected selected G1 mean
        accuracy = 1,                          # Within-family ranking accuracy
        progeny = list(                        # Explicit common production contract
            total.progeny = 90,                # Total produced DH descendants
            n.selected = 4,                    # Retained descendants per active family
            min.progeny = 30,                  # Equal families in this baseline
            max.progeny = 30,                  # Exactly thirty produced per family
            selection.method =                 # Finite-normal selection expectation
                "order.statistics",
            control = list(                    # Shared allocation settings
                algorithm = "auto"             # Qualified automatic integer allocator
            )
        ),
        control = list(                        # Family-set search settings
            method = "exact"                   # Bounded exhaustive family-set search
        )
    )
})
joint.equal
@

