The joint search returns the same three pairs and the same counts
$(30,29,31)$. This example therefore has no additional predicted gain from
changing the pairs after fixed-pair allocation. The exact outer search
confirms that allocation within the full fifteen-pair candidate graph,
subject to the stated numerical qualification. All original-input checks
pass. The cumulative work is shared across the evaluated family sets,
rather than reset for each proposal.

The following comparison uses \cde{st.cross.evaluate} to recompute the
three population summaries from their actual counts and stored scoring
method. Its additional digits make the small predicted allocation
difference visible.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
joint.equal.eval <- st.cross.evaluate(
    plan = joint.equal                         # Recomputed equal-family population
)
joint.fixed.eval <- st.cross.evaluate(
    plan = joint.fixed                         # Recomputed fixed-pair population
)
joint.plan.eval <- st.cross.evaluate(
    plan = joint.plan                          # Recomputed joint population
)
joint.metrics <- c("retained.baseline", "response", "retained.mean")
joint.comparison <- rbind(
    Equal = unlist(joint.equal.eval$population[joint.metrics]),
    Fixed = unlist(joint.fixed.eval$population[joint.metrics]),
    Joint = unlist(joint.plan.eval$population[joint.metrics])
)
colnames(joint.comparison) <- c("Baseline", "Response", "Selected")
joint.comparison <- cbind(
    joint.comparison,
    Seconds = c(joint.equal.time["elapsed"], joint.fixed.time["elapsed"],
                joint.plan.time["elapsed"])
)
joint.comparison
@

\begin{Verbatim}[frame=single,fontsize=\footnotesize,formatcom=\color{bl}]
      Baseline  Response Selected Seconds
Equal 2.414646 0.2111881 2.625834   0.003
Fixed 2.414646 0.2112196 2.625866   0.038
Joint 2.414646 0.2112196 2.625866   0.061
\end{Verbatim}

All three designs use ninety produced and twelve retained descendants,
with the same fitted moments, ranking accuracy and allowed graph. Moving
from equal counts to optimized counts increases the predicted selected
mean by approximately $3.1411\times10^{-5}$; the joint result matches the
fixed-pair result here. The improvement is wholly in the within-family
response because the pairs and retained weights remain the same.

The seconds column records one elapsed-time observation for each optimizer
call, excluding the shared data preparation and subsequent evaluation.
The values describe this small example. The genetic gain is a prediction
under the supplied finite-normal model; the simulation checks above and
the later selected-genomic and G2 models address separate biological
questions. The joint planner at this stage optimizes G1 with a fixed
retained count per active family.

\section{Allocation of produced and retained descendants}
\label{sec:retained-allocation}

The preceding joint planner retains four descendants from each selected
family. The \cde{total.selected} option instead fixes the total retained
population and allows its distribution across families to vary. Production
remains fixed at ninety descendants, and retention remains fixed at twelve.
Selection still acts within each family. A pooled selection of the twelve
highest scores across all ninety produced descendants is a different rule.

The comparison uses the same six maize founders, fifteen candidate pairs,
cross moments, ranking accuracy, finite-normal scoring and original kinship
reference as Section~\ref{sec:joint-progeny}. Each active family produces
between four and fifty descendants. Every family retains at least one
descendant, with the additional constraint that retention cannot exceed its
own production. The fixed-retention solution therefore remains feasible and
supplies a checked starting plan, including its actual produced and retained
counts. The family-slot parental and relatedness restrictions retain their
original meanings; no reproductive-role policy is added here.

The selected-individual weights are now $r_e/12$. A change in retention can
therefore change the retained-weight baseline as well as the within-family
selection response. The nominal retained founder shares can also differ
from family-slot shares. These differences are reported separately from
selected genomic ancestry, which requires the later selection-aware model.

The search below has explicit work limits. Its result may be a checked best
found plan with a remaining gap; the broader two-budget problem is not
qualified by the earlier fixed-retention optimum.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
retention.time <- system.time({
    retention.plan <- st.cross.plan(
        merit = cross.merit[practical.ids],    # The same six eligible maize founders
        K = practical.K,                       # Original-scale named kinship reference
        n.crosses = 3,                         # Three distinct produced families
        theta = max(diag(practical.K)),        # The same family-slot relatedness ceiling
        upper = 0.5,                           # The same family-slot parental bound
        scores = practical.scores,             # The same fifteen cross means and variances
        criterion = "usefulness",              # Retained-individual G1 mean
        accuracy = 1,                          # Identical within-family ranking accuracy
        progeny = list(                        # Fixed production and retention totals
            total.progeny = 90,                # Ninety produced DH descendants
            total.selected = 12,               # Twelve retained DH descendants
            min.progeny = 4,                   # Lower produced count for each active pair
            max.progeny = 50,                  # Upper produced count for each active pair
            min.selected = 1,                  # At least one retained from each family
            max.selected = 12,                 # Retained upper bound before r <= n
            selection.method =                 # Identical finite-normal scoring
                "order.statistics",
            control = list(                    # Cumulative allocation settings
                algorithm = "auto",            # Supported bounded integer allocation
                max.transitions = 1e7          # Shared work ceiling for the whole call
            )
        ),
        control = list(                        # Reproducible bounded family search
            method = "heuristic",              # Best-found search over family choices
            start = joint.plan,                # Actual produced and retained counts
            seed = 1,                          # Local search seed
            starts = 1,                        # One start from the checked incumbent
            maxit = 2000                       # Ceiling on outer search iterations
        )
    )
})
retention.plan
@

\begin{Verbatim}[frame=single,fontsize=\footnotesize,formatcom=\color{bl}]
