The selected pairs remain $1\times6$, $2\times6$ and $5\times6$, with
produced counts $(30,50,10)$ and retained counts $(3,8,1)$. Both budgets
and all original-input checks pass. The $2\times6$ family reaches the
produced upper bound of fifty and supplies eight of the twelve retained
descendants; $5\times6$ supplies one. The printed means therefore receive
weights $3/12$, $8/12$ and $1/12$, rather than equal family weights.

The retained-weight baseline increases to approximately 2.439239 while the
within-family response decreases to approximately 0.204619. Their sum,
approximately 2.643859, is greater than the fixed-retention prediction.
The higher baseline more than offsets the lower response; variable
retention does not improve the result merely by increasing every family's
selection intensity.

Status \cde{best.found} remains the optimizer's qualification. The
cumulative ten-million work ceiling is reached, and the reported global
upper bound leaves a gap of approximately 0.1074. This is the optimizer's
remaining estimated bound gap, not a measured biological prediction error.
The checked feasible incumbent is retained when that work limit stops
the search.

