% INTERPRETATION: future-genomics
The G2 genomic quantities use realized selected allele frequencies and
the original thirty-row reference. They are evaluated within each finite
replicate before averaging. Population gene diversity remains distinct
from individual DH heterozygosity, which is zero. Neither metric is a
guaranteed consequence of a current founder-contribution prediction.

The future-focused G2 panel has estimated genomic coancestry 0.46481
and gene diversity 0.11562. The G1-focused panel gives 0.46396 and
0.11604. These are fixed-reference finite-panel estimates; the objective
and current constraints do not impose a hard G2 genomic ceiling.

The same future objective can be assessed at declared G1 family-slot
relatedness limits. Each frontier point replaces \cde{theta} while
retaining the other current constraints and budgets. Independent starts
use the recorded standalone starting rules. One cumulative genetic-work
budget covers the entire call, including every point's final assessment.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
future.theta.limits <- c(0.25, 0.30, 0.35)
future.frontier.model <- future.model
future.frontier.model$future$comparators <- NULL
future.frontier.time <- system.time({
    future.frontier <- st.cross.frontier(
        merit = cross.merit[practical.ids],    # The same six candidate founders
        K = practical.K,                       # Original-scale fixed kinship reference
        n.crosses = 3,                         # Three current produced families
        theta = max(diag(practical.K)),        # Replaced by each declared theta limit
        upper = 0.5,                           # The same parental family-slot bound
        scores = practical.scores,             # The same fifteen actual candidate pairs
        criterion = "future",                  # Actual selected G2 training objective
        accuracy = 1,                          # The same current ranking accuracy
        progeny = future.progeny,              # The same current count domain and totals
        genetic.model = future.frontier.model, # Same core and banks; no extra comparators
        limits = future.theta.limits,          # Ordered G1 family-slot theta ceilings
        metric = "theta",                      # Axis concerns nominal G1 family slots
        warmstart = FALSE,                     # Each point keeps its standalone start
        control = list(                        # The same declared search schedule
            method = "heuristic",              # Bounded finite-bank search at each cap
            seed = 1,                          # The same recorded search seed
            starts = 1,                        # The same requested starting schedule
            maxit = 2000                       # Per-point outer iteration ceiling
        )
    )
})
future.frontier
@

\begin{Verbatim}[frame=single,fontsize=\footnotesize,formatcom=\color{bl}]
Gain-diversity frontier
Criterion: future; horizon: 2.0000; limit metric: theta
Points: 3; recorded search status applies to each point
  Point   Limit  Status      Feasible  Training G2  Held-out G2      SE
      1  0.2500  best.found  TRUE           2.7919       2.7893  0.0018
      2  0.3000  best.found  TRUE           2.7963       2.7930  0.0018
      3  0.3500  best.found  TRUE           2.7992       2.7926  0.0017

Actual held-out G2 metrics and bank completion
  Point  G2 genomic q  G2 gene diversity  Completed  Requested
      1        0.4478             0.1199   256.0000   256.0000
      2        0.4640             0.1160   256.0000   256.0000
      3        0.4648             0.1156   256.0000   256.0000
\end{Verbatim}

% INTERPRETATION: future-frontier
The horizontal limit concerns nominal G1 family-slot relatedness on the
original supplied kinship matrix. Actual selected G2 genomic coancestry
and gene diversity retain their separate estimates. An alternative
\cde{selected.founder.theta} axis would concern the qualified G1 UCPC
founder proxy and would also require that explicit label. Neither axis
is a hard bound on actual G2 genomic coancestry. Infeasible, unavailable
and work-limited points remain explicit; bounded-search and sample-based
points need not form a smooth or monotone tradeoff curve.

All three points complete their 256 held-out replicates, while all
retain \cde{best.found} and a training-limit reason. Their held-out
means are approximately 2.7893, 2.7930 and 2.7926 in the declared limit
order. The estimates are not monotone in the cap. Their individual SEs
do not supply a paired cross-point contrast or identify an optimum
between the displayed limits. The first and last points have estimated
G2 genomic coancestry approximately 0.4478 and 0.4648, respectively.

The immutable genetic core can be inherited for a new explicit future
assessment. Execution banks, comparator commands and historical G2
results are request-specific. Ordinary source evaluation without a new
future request executes no future bank and copies no stale assessment.
Planner starts supply counts and pairs rather than an implicit genetic
model. The forecast remains conditional on fitted marker effects and one
declared additional cycle; Monte Carlo precision does not establish field
performance or quantify marker-effect estimation uncertainty.

\appendix
\section{Mathematical definitions and implemented algorithms}
\label{app:cross-mathematics}

The definitions below distinguish parental merit, within-family segregation
variance, nominal parental contribution and covariance-based diversity.
All relationship matrices retain the reference population used to construct
them. Numerical convergence describes the stated model and supplied scores;
it does not establish the accuracy of the fitted marker effects.
Equation numbers below refer to the cited publications. The sample conventions,
constraints and numerical safeguards specific to SelectionTools are identified
separately from the established statistical formulas and algorithmic principles.

\subsection{Marker prediction, segregation variance and usefulness}

For the biallelic additive model, $z_{i\ell}\in\{0,1,2\}$ is the dosage
of the effect allele at marker $\ell$ in parent $i$, $a_0$ is the fitted
intercept and $a_\ell$ is its marker effect. The parental prediction and
expected cross mean are
\[
 g_i=a_0+\sum_\ell a_\ell z_{i\ell},\qquad
 \mu_{ij}=a_0+\frac12\sum_\ell a_\ell
                    (z_{i\ell}+z_{j\ell}).
\]
The cross mean is the dosage-notation form of Osthushenrich et al.\ (2017),
Equations~(1) and~(4). Thus $\mu_{ij}=(g_i+g_j)/2$ for the same additive model. The score table
retains the cross evaluator's supplied values; the planner does not replace
them by values reconstructed from exported parental predictions.

For two completely homozygous parents, the marker contribution difference
is $\delta_{ij,\ell}=a_\ell(z_{i\ell}-z_{j\ell})/2$. The linked
segregation variance used for DH or completely homozygous SSD lines is
\[
 v_{ij}=\sum_{c}\ \sum_{\ell,k\ \mathrm{on}\ c}
       \rho_{\ell k}\,\delta_{ij,\ell}\delta_{ij,k},
 \qquad \rho_{\ell k}=2q_{\ell k}-1.
\]
This covariance contraction rewrites Osthushenrich et al.\ (2017),
Equations~(2) and~(13), using the matching-origin relation in their
Equation~(16). The sum includes both orders of each marker pair and the diagonal.
Different chromosomes have zero covariance. Here $q_{\ell k}$ is the
probability that the two loci have matching parental origin. With
recombination frequency $r$ and $t$ additional random-intermating generations,
the implemented correlations are
\[
 \rho_{\mathrm{DH}}(r,t)=(1-2r)(1-r)^t,\qquad
 \rho_{\mathrm{SSD}}(r,t)=\frac{1-2r}{1+2r}(1-r)^t.
\]
The DH and SSD correlations are $2q-1$ from Osthushenrich et al.\ (2017),
Equations~(20) and~(23), respectively; their Equations~(19) and~(22) give
the underlying linkage disequilibria. The SSD expression concerns the limit under repeated selfing; $t$ is not
the number of selfing generations. The map distance $d$ in Morgans gives
$r=(1-\exp(-2d))/2$ under Haldane or $r=\tanh(2d)/2$ under Kosambi.
The first is the inverse of Osthushenrich et al.\ (2017), Equation~(24),
following Haldane (1919). The second is Kosambi (1943), Equation~(6), p.~173.
The unlinked inbred-parent calculation reduces to $\sum_\ell\delta_{ij,\ell}^2$.
The C implementation forms the chromosome-specific recombination and
origin-probability matrices and contracts them with the parental marker
contributions, following Osthushenrich et al.\ (2017).

Normal truncation and its finite-family correction follow Burrows (1972,
pp.~1091--1100). For retention fraction $p\in(0,1)$, normal truncation intensity is
\[
 i(p)=\frac{\phi\{\Phi^{-1}(1-p)\}}{p},
\]
where $\phi$ and $\Phi$ are the standard normal density and distribution
function. With $R$ retained individuals from $G$ produced individuals,
the implemented Burrows (1972) finite-family approximation is
\[
 i_B(R,G)=i(R/G)-
 \frac{1-R/G}{2R(1+1/G)i(R/G)},\qquad 1\leq R<G.
\]
The boundary definitions are $i(1)=i_B(G,G)=0$. Family usefulness with
selection accuracy $a\in[0,1]$ is
\[
 U_{ij}=\mu_{ij}+a\,i_B(R,G)\sqrt{v_{ij}}.
\]
The usefulness criterion of Schnell and Utz (1975) combines the family mean
and a within-family selection differential, as described by Osthushenrich et al.\ (2017,
Introduction, pp.~1--2). Zhong and Jannink (2007, Introduction) discuss the
corresponding superior-progeny criterion. The accuracy-scaled form above also appears in
Fern\'andez-Gonz\'alez et al.\ (2026), Equation~(16), with the intensity
here supplied by the Burrows calculation. The SD, rather than the variance, enters the differential. The infinite
population option uses $i(p)$ instead. The fixed-score planner uses
$\mu_{ij}+a(es_{ij}-\mu_{ij})$ from its common supplied table. Changing its
common progeny count does not recompute $es_{ij}$. Variable-size allocation
uses $\mu_{ij}$ and $v_{ij}$ to recompute the differential for each size.

\subsection{Parental contributions and equal-sized integer families}

For marker frequencies $p_\ell$ in the fixed reference, the genomic
relationship matrix in this example is
\[
 W_{i\ell}=z_{i\ell}-2p_\ell,\qquad
 A=\frac{WW'}{2\sum_\ell p_\ell(1-p_\ell)},\qquad K=A/2.
\]
This is the first genomic relationship construction of VanRaden (2008,
p.~4416; unnumbered first-method formula), written for dosage coding
$0,1,2$ rather than $-1,0,1$. The denominator must be positive.
No diagonal adjustment is applied.
The matrix can be singular and its off-diagonal entries can be negative.
The quadratic form $Q(c)=c'Kc$ is a reference-dependent group-relatedness
index. Dividing $A$ by two does not itself give an absolute probability
of identity by descent; Endelman (2025, Discussion following Equation~(22))
also distinguishes genomic correlations from identity-by-descent probabilities.

The merit and coancestry terms in optimum contribution selection follow
Meuwissen (1997), Equations~[1] and~[3], with $K=A/2$. His sex-specific
normalizations in Equation~[2] become one simplex for the supplied plant-parent
pool here, with the stated lower and upper bounds. The problem is
\[
 \max_c g'c\quad\text{subject to}\quad
 \mathbf1'c=1,\quad l\leq c\leq u,\quad Q(c)\leq\theta.
\]
The inner C solver minimizes $Q(c)-2t g'c$ over the box-constrained
simplex. This scalar tradeoff has the Lagrangian form of Meuwissen (1997),
Equation~[4]; the reduced-coordinate solver described here is the
SelectionTools implementation. An active-set method uses a basis of free directions whose
components sum to zero, an eigendecomposition of the reduced quadratic
form, and a bounded line search. Rank-deficient directions and active
bounds receive explicit treatment. The outer search brackets and bisects
the merit--relatedness tradeoff. A separate minimum-relatedness problem
tests feasibility; the minimum-relatedness boundary also has an affine
rank-based calculation.

For any candidate $x$, $\lambda\geq0$, and box-constrained simplex
$\mathit{C}$, convexity gives the merit upper bound
\[
 g'c\leq \lambda\{\theta+Q(x)\}
       +\max_{y\in\mathit{C}}(g-2\lambda Kx)'y.
\]
The remaining maximization is a bounded linear allocation. Feasibility
residuals and the difference between such a bound and the achieved merit
govern the reported numerical qualification; an iteration limit does not
establish an optimum. The displayed bound follows by applying the first-order
convexity inequality of Boyd and Vandenberghe (2004), Equation~(3.2), p.~69,
to $Q$ and using $Q(c)\leq\theta$.

For $M$ equal-sized distinct non-selfed families, $x_e\in\{0,1\}$ selects
an allowed unordered pair $e=(i_e,j_e)$. The parental uses and nominal
gene contributions are
\[
 \sum_e x_e=M,\qquad
 d_i=\sum_e x_e\{\mathbf1(i_e=i)+\mathbf1(j_e=i)\},\qquad
 c_i=\frac{d_i}{2M}.
\]
Joint planning maximizes $M^{-1}\sum_e x_e h_e$, with $h_e$ the additive
cross mean or supplied usefulness, subject to contribution bounds and
$Q(c)\leq\theta$. Without cross scores its mean objective equals $g'c$.
Fixed-contribution planning instead minimizes
$M^{-1}\sum_e x_e K_{i_ej_e}$ while preserving every $d_i$.
This is the kinship-minimizing mate-allocation objective described by Wellmann
(2019, section ``Mate allocation''; unnumbered objective), with the distinct-pair
and integer-usage conventions stated here. A continuous
contribution vector is therefore insufficient unless all its uses are
integer-realizable in the allowed distinct-pair graph.

The exact search uses the branch-and-bound principle of Land and Doig
(1960, Section~3), implemented here by include/exclude branches in score order.
Available incident edges and remaining parental capacity prune impossible
branches; sums of the best remaining edge scores bound objective values.
Continuous contribution relaxations provide additional bounds when
applicable. Automatic method selection compares an estimated enumeration
workload with the node budget. The alternative heuristic combines multiple
initial constructions with local edge changes and a local random-number
stream. Fixed-contribution changes rewire two or three pairs while
preserving all parental uses. Every retained incumbent is rechecked from
its complete selected pairs and original matrix. The exact method can
establish optimality after a completed search; a feasible heuristic
incumbent has status \cde{best.found}. An unresolved boundary or exhausted
budget does not establish infeasibility. Relatedness is recomputed using
integer uses and the original matrix, with numerical allowance
\[
 \epsilon\max\left\{|\theta|,\sum_{i,j}|K_{ij}|c_ic_j\right\},
\]
where $\epsilon$ is \cde{control\$tolerance}. Its computed roundoff
enclosure must satisfy the bound including this allowance. A zero limit
additionally requires a row-residual check independent of $\epsilon$.
These tolerance, roundoff and status rules are implementation safeguards.

\subsection{Continuous optimum mate allocation}

The continuous model follows the diploid mate-allocation framework of
Endelman (2025, section ``Convex optimization''). For allowed pairs $e$,
mating fractions $x_e$ are continuous and sum to one. The parental incidence matrix has entries
$B_{ie}=\{\mathbf1(i_e=i)+\mathbf1(j_e=i)\}/2$, so $c=Bx$.
This is the allowed-pair incidence representation of Endelman (2025),
Equation~(8). A self mating, when present in this interface, contributes one to its
parent's incidence entry. With supplied mating scores $h_e$,
\cde{st.oma} maximizes $h'x$ under the supplied bounds on $x$ and $c$.
Its two kinship quantities are
\[
 F_1=\sum_e x_eK_{i_ej_e},\qquad F_2=(Bx)'K(Bx).
\]
These are the diploid specializations of Endelman (2025), Equations~(14)
and~(19), with random mating in the following generation for $F_2$.
The complete supplied $N\times N$ matrix defines the fixed reference
\[
 F_0=\frac1N\sum_i(2K_{ii}-1)\quad\text{for \cde{base="current"}},
 \qquad
 F_0=\frac1{N^2}\sum_{i,j}K_{ij}\quad\text{for \cde{base="RM"}}.
\]
The current-population expression follows the diploid form of Endelman
(2025), Equation~(22), with $A=2K$. The random-mating reference specializes
his Equation~(16) to uniform contributions. Using the complete supplied
reference before restriction to eligible parents is the stated interface
convention. The reference must be below one. For \cde{dF=c(dL,dU)}, the constraints are
\begin{align*}
 F_0+(1-F_0)d_L&\leq F_1\leq F_0+(1-F_0)d_U,\\
 F_2&\leq F_0+(1-F_0)d_U(2-d_U).
\end{align*}
A scalar \cde{dF} sets an equality for $F_1$. The reported changes are
\[
 dF_1=\frac{F_1-F_0}{1-F_0},\qquad
 dF_2=1-\sqrt{\frac{1-F_2}{1-F_0}}.
\]
The bounds and rate expressions are algebraic rearrangements of Endelman
(2025), the one- and two-generation recurrences in Equations~(10)--(11).
The linear $F_1$ and quadratic $F_2$ restrictions remain distinct.

The C implementation uses a primal--dual interior-point method with
the predictor--corrector principle of Mehrotra (1992).
Positive inequality slacks and multipliers enter the perturbed
complementarity equations. An affine predictor determines the centering
parameter; the corrector includes the predictor's second-order product.
A fraction-to-boundary rule and residual line search control the update.
The Newton calculation uses a symmetric system of parental dimension,
with the quadratic-constraint multiplier represented explicitly, and
residual refinement. Scaling and rank decisions aid this calculation;
feasibility is checked against the original supplied matrix and limits.
Separate merit bounds and constraint residuals govern the status.
The parent-dimension Newton formulation, residual checks and status
qualification describe its implementation for this problem. Continuous mating
fractions are not rounded into integer families.

\subsection{Covariance-based proportional loss of SD}

The \cde{propsd} evaluator follows the published PropSD estimator of
Fern\'andez-Gonz\'alez et al.\ (2026), Equations~(14)--(15) and
Appendix~C, Equations~(C1)--(C6). For the complete reference covariance-proportional matrix
$G$, the model is $u\sim N(0,\tau^2G)$. A literal selected sample
$s=(s_1,\ldots,s_m)$ has covariance $G_s=G[s,s]$. Repeated identifiers
repeat the same random genetic value; they do not create independent
copies. The marker-frequency reference and the complete $G$ remain fixed.

For any $n\times n$ covariance matrix $V$, $n\geq2$, the centered matrix
and ordinary sample variance with denominator $n-1$ are
\[
 H_n=I_n-\mathbf1\mathbf1'/n,\qquad C=H_nVH_n,
 \qquad S^2=\frac{z'H_nz}{n-1},\quad z\sim N(0,V).
\]
The standard Gaussian quadratic-form identities of Magnus (1978),
Lemmas~2.2--2.3, pp.~203--204, applied to $H_n/(n-1)$, give the exact moments
\[
 \mu_V=E(S^2)=\frac{\operatorname{tr}(C)}{n-1},\qquad
 v_V=\operatorname{Var}(S^2)
     =\frac{2\sum_{i,j}C_{ij}^2}{(n-1)^2}.
\]
These are also Fern\'andez-Gonz\'alez et al.\ (2026), Equation~(15)
and Appendix~C, Equations~(C1)--(C4), in centered-matrix notation. Their
two-moment Gamma approximation, Equation~(C5), has shape
$\alpha_V=\mu_V^2/v_V$ and scale $\beta_V=v_V/\mu_V$. Its half moment
approximates the expected sample SD:
\[
 e(V)=\sqrt{\beta_V}\,
       \frac{\Gamma(\alpha_V+1/2)}{\Gamma(\alpha_V)},\qquad
 \widehat{\mathrm{PropSD}}=1-\frac{e(G_s)}{e(G)}.
\]
The Gamma half moment follows from Euler's integral (NIST DLMF,
Equation~5.2.1), as worked out in Fern\'andez-Gonz\'alez et al.\ (2026),
Equation~(C6) and its following simplification. The common $\tau$ cancels. A general Gaussian quadratic form is a
weighted sum of chi-squares, so Gamma moment matching is an approximation;
it is exact when all nonzero eigenvalues of $C$ are equal, including
rank-one contrasts. The estimator is a ratio of expected SDs, rather
than the expectation of a random ratio.

Integer multiplicities avoid materializing a replicated covariance
matrix. With counts $k_i\geq0$, $m=\sum_i k_i\geq2$, $w=k/m$,
$r=Gw$, and $D_{ij}=G_{ij}-r_i-r_j+w'Gw$, the same moments are
\[
 \mu_s=\frac{\sum_i k_iD_{ii}}{m-1},\qquad
 v_s=\frac{2\sum_{i,j}k_ik_jD_{ij}^2}{(m-1)^2}.
\]
These count expressions are an algebraic regrouping of Equation~(15)
of Fern\'andez-Gonz\'alez et al.\ (2026), with identical repeated-parent
rows grouped together. Only positive-count parents enter the selected-sample sums. The total
$m$ matters as well as the normalized counts. Continuous contribution
fractions alone therefore do not specify this statistic.

The C evaluator checks positive semidefiniteness. Each reference or
selected sample uses its own active parental support to form anchored
contrasts directly from the original supplied covariance entries, followed
by occurrence-weighted centering and local contrast scaling. This preserves
small selected-sample contrasts even when the reference contains much larger
variances. The reference covariance model remains fixed; the local numerical
scaling does not reconstruct marker frequencies. Extended-precision moment
sums and separate reference and selected scale factors reduce cancellation
and overflow. Moments reported in original covariance units can still exceed
the numeric range of R. The SD calculation is
\[
 e(V)=\sqrt{\mu_V}\exp\{\ell(\alpha_V)\},\qquad
 \ell(\alpha)=\log\Gamma(\alpha+1/2)-\log\Gamma(\alpha)
                    -\tfrac12\log\alpha.
\]
For $\alpha>128$, the implemented large-shape expansion is
$\ell(\alpha)\simeq-1/(8\alpha)+1/(192\alpha^3)-1/(640\alpha^5)$;
these coefficients specialize NIST DLMF, Equation~5.11.8, by subtracting
the $h=0$ expansion from the $h=1/2$ expansion. The cutoff at 128 is an
implementation choice; smaller shapes use the log-Gamma functions. A positive reference contrast
variance is required. A selected sample with zero contrast variance has
$e(G_s)=0$ and loss one; a one-occurrence sample has undefined sample SD.
Negative loss is retained when the selected expected SD exceeds the
reference expected SD. Common positive covariance scaling and addition
of a common random-intercept covariance leave the ratio unchanged when
the covariance matrices remain valid.

The optional cross-planner convention \cde{sample="family.slots"}
assigns one occurrence to each parental slot of every selected family,
so $k_i=d_i$ and $m=2M$. The complete supplied diversity matrix defines
the reference, including any additional reference parents. The constraint
is the strict comparison
$\widehat{\mathrm{PropSD}}<b$, where $b$ is \cde{diversity\$max}.
The direction and strictness of this cutoff follow
Fern\'andez-Gonz\'alez et al.\ (2026), Equation~(18).
It constrains the computed Gamma estimate; it is not an interval proof
of biological diversity loss. Trait merits, $c'Kc$ and realized genomic ancestry after
within-family selection remain different quantities.
The exact search checks the criterion at complete candidate plans;
objective bounds obtained by omitting it remain valid relaxations.
The joint heuristic includes both relatedness and diversity violations
when seeking feasibility and rechecks the criterion on retained plans.
Fixed-contribution rewiring leaves the family-slot multiplicities, and
hence this diversity criterion, unchanged.

\subsection{Integer progeny allocation for fixed families}

The produced counts $G_e$ vary while parent pairs and retained counts
$R_e$ remain fixed. With total budget $T$, the integer constraints are
\[
 L_e\leq G_e\leq U_e,\qquad G_e\geq R_e\geq1,\qquad
 \sum_eG_e=T.
\]
Repeated unordered pairs are pooled into one biological family before
selection intensity is calculated. Their retained counts and produced
bounds are summed, and they have one common prediction accuracy $a_e$.
The retained-population mean is
\[
 \overline U(G)=\sum_e\frac{R_e}{R_+}
       \{\mu_e+a_e\sqrt{v_e}\,i_B(R_e,G_e)\},\qquad
 R_+=\sum_eR_e.
\]
For equal retained counts this is the mean-family usefulness objective of
Fern\'andez-Gonz\'alez et al.\ (2026), Equation~(17). The displayed
retained-count weights and pooling convention define the allocation problem
implemented here. The weighting follows retained individuals rather than produced counts.
The constant $\sum_eR_e\mu_e/R_+$ is excluded from allocation comparisons,
which protects small response differences against large common merit
offsets.

For family response utility
$u_e(g)=(R_e/R_+)a_e\sqrt{v_e}\,i_B(R_e,g)$, exact dynamic programming
uses the finite-stage principle of optimality of Bellman (1954,
Section~2 and the basic recurrence in Equation~(3.3), pp.~504--505),
specialized to the remaining progeny budget:
\[
 D_0(0)=0,\quad D_0(b\ne0)=-\infty,\qquad
 D_e(b)=\max_{L_e\leq g\leq U_e}
             \{D_{e-1}(b-g)+u_e(g)\}.
\]
The C recurrence works with additional counts above the family minima.
Reachable budget ranges restrict states. Two objective rows and a
family-by-budget traceback table recover the integer allocation.
With $T'=T-\sum_eL_e$, the worst-case time is $O(M(T'+1)W)$ for maximum
count-range width $W$; traceback storage is $O(M(T'+1))$. No concavity
assumption is needed for this enumeration of feasible counts. The shared
C intensity kernel also serves the existing finite-family cross
evaluator. Upward-rounded recurrence sums give an upper bound on the
finite computed utility table; a downward sum for the traced allocation
gives the corresponding numerical gap. These bounds do not make the
normal-selection or Burrows approximation exact. A completed calculation
reports \cde{optimal} only when its gap satisfies the numerical tolerance;
an unresolved gap has status \cde{numerically.unresolved}. A resource cap
retains a deterministic feasible allocation with \cde{resource.limit}
and an infinite unresolved bound.

The same diploid parental-incidence relation as Endelman (2025),
Equation~(8), gives the nominal retained and produced shares separately
\[
 c_i^{\mathrm{ret}}=\frac{\sum_eR_e
       \{\mathbf1(i_e=i)+\mathbf1(j_e=i)\}}{2R_+},\qquad
 c_i^{\mathrm{prod}}=\frac{\sum_eG_e
       \{\mathbf1(i_e=i)+\mathbf1(j_e=i)\}}{2T}.
\]
The inherited parental bounds and relatedness limit apply to
$c^{\mathrm{ret}}$. Both relatedness values are reported; the produced
one is a diagnostic in this allocation model. A family-slot PropSD
constraint remains fixed because the parent pairs remain fixed. Neither
nominal vector predicts the genomic ancestry of selected descendants.
The optimum is conditional on the supplied pairs and retained counts,
rather than a joint global optimum over pairs and progeny resources.

\subsection{Finite-normal order statistics and discrete allocation}

For independent standard-normal observations, $Z_{(r:g)}$ denotes the
$r$th smallest value in a sample of size $g$. The optional
\cde{selection.method="order.statistics"} replaces $i_B(R,g)$ by
\[
 A(g,R)=\frac1R\sum_{r=g-R+1}^{g}E\{Z_{(r:g)}\},\qquad A(R,R)=0.
\]
Writing $\phi$ and $\Phi$ for the standard-normal density and distribution
function, respectively, the expectations are
\[
 E\{Z_{(r:g)}\}=\frac{g!}{(r-1)!(g-r)!}
 \int_{-\infty}^{\infty}z\phi(z)
       \Phi(z)^{r-1}\{1-\Phi(z)\}^{g-r}\,dz.
\]
These are the finite-normal order-statistic and selected-mean definitions
in Okoye et al.\ (2026), Equations~(4)--(8). With fixed selected counts,
the implemented objective is
\[
 \overline U_{\mathrm{normal}}(G)=
 \sum_e\frac{R_e}{R_+}
       \{\mu_e+a_e\sqrt{v_e}\,A(G_e,R_e)\}.
\]
The factor $a_e$ is the within-family selection accuracy. For phenotypic
selection with heritability $H_e^2$, it equals $H_e$. The expectation is
exact for the stated independent normal-sampling model; numerical
integration and a finite-locus genetic population remain separate
qualifications.

The integer allocator uses the actual finite increment
\[
 \Delta_e(g)=\frac{R_e}{R_+}a_e\sqrt{v_e}
             \{A(g+1,R_e)-A(g,R_e)\}.
\]
$T_g$ denotes the $R$th greatest observation among the original $g$ draws,
and $X$ is an independent additional draw. The increase in the sum of the
$R$ greatest observations is $(X-T_g)_+$. Under a coupling that preserves
the original observations, $T_{g+1}\geq T_g$, so its expected positive
increment is nonnegative and decreases as $g$ increases. Equivalently,
with $Q(z)=1-\Phi(z)$,
\[
 A(g+1,R)-A(g,R)=\frac1R\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
 Q(z)\Pr\{\operatorname{Bin}(g,Q(z))\leq R-1\}\,dz.
\]
This is the discrete diminishing-return property underlying the
monotonicity argument of Okoye et al.\ (2026), Appendix~2,
Equations~(10)--(20). Starting from each lower bound $L_e$, selection of
the greatest available $\Delta_e(g)$ gives a global optimum of this
separable integer allocation problem: each family's decreasing increment
sequence is selected in order, with upper bounds truncating the sequence.
Ties can yield several optimal allocations. Continuous derivatives of an
extension of $A(g,R)$ are not generally equal to these one-individual
increments; the implementation ranks finite increments.

The fixed-retention C evaluator uses beta-quantile transformations of these integrals and
adaptive quadrature split at the midpoint of the unit interval. For $g>R$,
the nonnegative score representation is
\[
 A(g,R)=\frac{g}{R}
 E_{B\sim\operatorname{Beta}(R,g-R)}
     [\phi\{\Phi^{-1}(B)\}].
\]
Normal symmetry gives
$A(g,R)=(g-R)A(g,g-R)/R$, reducing near-full-retention calculations to
the smaller selected count. Direct finite-increment evaluation avoids
subtracting nearly equal selected means. A maximum heap retains the
currently greatest family increment, and cached evaluations serve repeated
count and retained-count combinations.
The \cde{greedy} allocator uses this discrete rule and \cde{dp} uses the
same normal-scoring objective in the budget recurrence above.
The \cde{auto} option starts with greedy allocation and can use the same
objective in dynamic programming when the estimated gap remains unresolved
and the resource limits permit it. Normal integrals and tail calculations
are evaluated numerically. Their error estimates and floating-point uncertainty
qualify the reported gap. Status \cde{numerically.optimal} identifies an
estimated gap meeting tolerance, rather than an interval-certified bound
for the exact normal integrals. Otherwise the result reports
\cde{numerically.unresolved} or \cde{resource.limit}, as appropriate.
An order-statistic request never changes silently to Burrows scoring.
The discrete theorem requires fixed pairs, fixed retained counts, equal
cost per produced individual and separable count bounds. It does not
extend automatically to coupled produced-share constraints or joint
optimization of pairs and resources.

\subsection{Common population evaluation}

The evaluator pools repeated unordered parent pairs before scoring their
produced counts $G_e$ and retained counts $R_e$. Stored or explicitly
supplied means, variances and accuracies must agree within a pooled family.
The totals are $T=\sum_eG_e$ and $R_+=\sum_eR_e$. A family with $R_e=0$ has no
weight in the selected summaries and no selection intensity is evaluated
for it. With $T>0$, the produced-family weights and marginal moments are
\[
 p_e=\frac{G_e}{T},\qquad M_0=\sum_ep_e\mu_e,\qquad
 W_0=\sum_ep_ev_e,
\]
\[
 B_0=\sum_ep_e(\mu_e-M_0)^2,\qquad V_0=W_0+B_0.
\]
This law-of-total-variance decomposition describes the genetic value of
one uniformly sampled produced individual under the supplied family
moments. It is not the expected empirical variance of all produced
individuals or a segregation variance for an additional breeding cycle.

With $R_+>0$, the retained-count baseline and G1 mean are
\[
 M_{1,\mathrm{base}}=\sum_e\frac{R_e}{R_+}\mu_e,\qquad
 J_1=\sum_{e:R_e>0}\frac{R_e}{R_+}U_e,
 \qquad R_1=J_1-M_{1,\mathrm{base}},
\]
where $U_e$ uses the explicitly resolved Burrows or finite-normal method.
Thus $R_1$ retains the same family weights in the selected mean and its
baseline. A difference from $M_0$, whose weights are $G_e/T$, is a separate
contrast. The available selected between-family term is
\[
 B_1=\sum_{e:R_e>0}\frac{R_e}{R_+}(U_e-J_1)^2.
\]
The analytic evaluator leaves selected within-family and total marginal
variance unavailable. Explicit genotype scenarios in
Section~\ref{sec:selected-genotypes} estimate separately defined finite-genotype
quantities under the declared model. They require second moments of the selected normal
order statistics and a declared joint genetic-value/selection-score model.
The expected empirical variance of the finite retained set further
requires covariances among selected individuals. For $R_+>1$ its identity
would be
\[
 E(S_1^2)=\frac{R_+}{R_+-1}
       \{V_{1,\mathrm{marginal}}-\operatorname{Var}(\overline G_1)\}.
\]
The currently unavailable covariance terms are not replaced by an
independence assumption. For $R_+=1$ sample variance is undefined.
Zero total production or retention similarly yields unavailable summaries
with an explicit reason rather than an assumed zero genetic value.

For $C$ distinct produced families, the additional family-based nominal
share is
\[
 c_i^{\mathrm{family}}=\frac1{2C}
    \sum_{e:G_e>0}\{\mathbf1(i_e=i)+\mathbf1(j_e=i)\}.
\]
The produced and retained shares remain as defined above. Each reported
relatedness is $c'Kc$ for its stated basis on the complete original named
reference, with zero shares for unused reference parents. Missing kinship
input leaves relatedness unavailable. Inherited constraints retain their
declared basis; family-slot diversity retains the complete supplied
reference and its parental-occurrence convention.

Normalization, numerical scoring and centered accumulation of the mixture
moments are C-owned. The evaluator independently recomputes supported
budget, bound, contribution, relatedness and diversity residuals from the
input plan. Evaluation status describes those checks and is separate from
the source optimizer's convergence or optimality status. Counts are not
repaired during evaluation.

Future-oriented allocation across breeding cycles, as investigated through
simulation by Hamazaki and Iwata (2024), requires a further declared mating
and selection policy. Here G2 means crosses between selected G1 DH lines
followed by new DH derivation and selection. Current family means,
segregation variances and parental kinship alone do not identify the
selected genomic state needed for this forecast. Selected genomic ancestry,
selected genomic diversity and G2 merit therefore remain unavailable in
the initial evaluator.

\subsection{Reproductive roles and practical constraints}

Parent-count limits, role-specific restrictions, equal parental use and
contribution capacities are established mate-selection choices; examples
are described by Gorjanc and Hickey (2018). The associated
\href{https://github.com/AlphaGenes/AlphaMate/blob/main/doc/AlphaMate.txt}{manual}
groups them under ``Mating/Crossing constraints''.
Its parent-count sections are ``NumberOfParents'', ``NumberOfMaleParents''
and ``NumberOfFemaleParents''. Equal use is described in
``EqualizeContributions'', while ``LimitContributionsMin'' and
``LimitContributionsMax'' specify contribution bounds.
These manual sections have no numbered formulas. The equations below
specify the incidence bases, pooling conventions and C search implemented
in SelectionTools.

The biological response key remains an unordered nonself parent pair
$e=\{i,j\}$. An identified directed record $a=(i_{\mathrm{seed}},
j_{\mathrm{pollen}})$ additionally records its reproductive roles.
Equivalent reciprocal records pool their produced and retained counts
before selection response is evaluated. Different directions do not
create independent selection opportunities within the same biological
family under this direction-neutral score model. The equal-family planner
chooses at most one eligible direction for each selected biological pair.
Explicit eligibility and allowed-pair restrictions intersect.

For basis $b$ equal to produced, retained or operations, $w_a^b$ is
the produced count, retained count or explicitly supplied operation count,
respectively. With $T_b=\sum_aw_a^b$, the role-specific incidences are
\[
 S_i^b=\sum_{a:\mathrm{seed}(a)=i}w_a^b,\qquad
 L_i^b=\sum_{a:\mathrm{pollen}(a)=i}w_a^b,\qquad
 D_i^b=S_i^b+L_i^b.
\]
The overall and role-normalized nominal shares have different denominators:
\[
 c_i^b=\frac{D_i^b}{2T_b},\qquad
 h_i^{b,\mathrm{seed}}=\frac{S_i^b}{T_b},\qquad
 h_i^{b,\mathrm{pollen}}=\frac{L_i^b}{T_b}.
\]
Each role share sums to one. Pool shares sum these values over the pool
without re-normalizing within that pool. A produced-use limit counts
descendant slots, while operations count only explicitly recorded crossing
events. Operation counts are not inferred from produced counts. A zero
basis total leaves its shares and relatedness unavailable.

For family slots, each distinct produced biological family contributes
one parental incidence at each end. Its overall denominator is twice the
number of those families. Role-specific family slots require a single
unambiguous direction per family. A pooled family with multiple directions
instead needs identified produced, retained or operation accounting for
a role-specific restriction.

For a selector of pool $\mathcal P$ and role $t$, $Q_i^{b,t}$ equals
$D_i^b$, $S_i^b$ or $L_i^b$ for all, seed or pollen use. Distinct used
parents, aggregate uses and aggregate shares are, respectively,
\[
 N_{\mathcal P,t}^b=\sum_{i\in\mathcal P}\mathbf1(Q_i^{b,t}>0),\qquad
 U_{\mathcal P,t}^b=\sum_{i\in\mathcal P}Q_i^{b,t},\qquad
 H_{\mathcal P,t}^b=\sum_{i\in\mathcal P}v_i^{b,t},
\]
where $v=c$ for all roles and $v=h$ for a specific role. Each limits row
places inclusive lower and upper bounds on one of these quantities.
Equal use requires the positive $Q_i^{b,t}$ values in the selected subset
to be identical integers. It does not force every eligible parent to be
used. Exact parent counts use equal lower and upper integer bounds.

Count accumulation and feasibility comparisons use checked 64-bit integer
arithmetic. Diagnostic decimal strings retain exact achieved counts and
residuals; numeric fields become unavailable when such integers exceed
the exact binary64 reporting range. A share constraint compares an integer
numerator $q$ with the actual represented numeric bound on denominator $d$:
\[
 \lceil\ell d\rceil\leq q\leq\lfloor u d\rfloor.
\]
The product boundaries use portable two-limb integer arithmetic, preserving
the represented binary64 bounds rather than their rounded printed values.
Integer equality has no floating-point tolerance. Relatedness calculations
retain their separate numerical uncertainty.

Inherited and explicit requests remain separate intersecting restrictions.
The direction map is joined to the actual current family rows and their
counts, so saved counts do not override an edited plan. The common checker
recomputes every supported request from these inputs. A failed check is
reported without repairing the plan; unavailable or unresolved checks do
not count as passes.

Role-aware exact cross selection extends the bounded family search with
eligible direction choices and biological-pair exclusivity. A fixed-pair
allocation with coupled produced-use, share, relatedness or diversity
constraints requires the constrained integer path. Separable increments
can bound its objective, but their unconstrained optimum is not a
certificate for the constrained feasible set. A completed search, valid
bounds and qualified numerical comparisons determine the optimum claim.
Exhausted work limits retain an honestly qualified result rather than a
capacity-violating fallback or a false infeasibility proof.

The family-based nominal PropSD sample is \cde{family.\allowbreak slots}.
Produced counts define \cde{produced.\allowbreak founder.\allowbreak slots},
while retained counts define
\cde{retained.\allowbreak founder.\allowbreak slots}.
All three retain the complete named reference matrix and literal integer parental
multiplicities. Produced and retained founder-slot totals are $2T$ and
$2R_+$, respectively, within the supported literal-count domain. Each
strict computed loss limit intersects any inherited family-slot limit.
These nominal founder samples do not identify selected-DH genomic
diversity or ancestry.

\subsection{Joint family activation and produced counts}

For allowed unordered biological families $e$, activation $y_e\in\{0,1\}$
and nonnegative integer produced count $n_e$ satisfy
\[
 \sum_e y_e=C,\qquad \sum_e n_e=P,\qquad
 L y_e\le n_e\le U y_e,\qquad r_e=r y_e,\quad L\ge r>0.
\]
Consequently the retained budget is $R=Cr$. With reproductive roles,
exactly one eligible directed alternative is chosen for each active
biological family. Reciprocal alternatives retain the same biological
mean and variance and do not create another selection opportunity.
For selection intensity $a(n,r)$ defined above, the two supported
criteria are
\[
 J_{\mathrm{mean}}=\frac1C\sum_e y_e\mu_e,\qquad
 J_{G1}=\frac1C\sum_{e:y_e=1}
       \{\mu_e+\rho\sqrt{v_e}\,a(n_e,r)\}.
\]
These are retained-count weights $r/R$, rather than produced-count
weights $n_e/P$. The finite-family response follows the formulas and
qualification in the preceding order-statistic subsection; the joint
activation, resource constraints and search described here specify the
SelectionTools implementation. The future-oriented progeny-allocation
topic is also investigated by Hamazaki and Iwata (2024), whose
simulation-based methodology does not define this G1 search.

The planner's parental contribution limits and $c'Kc\le\theta$ continue
to use $c_i=d_i/(2C)$, where $d_i$ is the number of active families
containing parent $i$. They are independent of $n_e$. Additional practical
constraints retain their explicitly declared family, produced or retained
basis and the complete original relationship reference. Common retained
counts make family and retained shares coincide here; their provenance
remains separate. Operation counts are not inferred from descendant
production.


The exact C search branches over biological family choices and eligible
directions, with the original parental and practical constraints. A
fixed-family subproblem allocates the produced budget. Its separable
finite-normal path uses the discrete increments above; the Burrows path
uses the corresponding bounded dynamic program. Coupled produced-use,
share or diversity restrictions require their checked constrained search.
The branch-and-bound principle follows Land and Doig (1960); the particular
family graph, resource model, bounds and numerical qualification are the
implementation described here. An exact fixed-family allocation does not
establish an optimum over family choices by itself.

The family-search bound permits each candidate its greatest supported
response independently, relaxing the shared production budget and
practical restrictions. The finite-normal bound uses its qualified
monotone count endpoint and estimated numerical error. A Burrows bound
uses an enumerated count range; if no justified finite bound is available,
the upper bound remains unknown. This relaxation can be loose, but it
does not replace the actual-count objective or make a failed constraint
feasible. Completed branch exclusion and estimated numerical comparisons
determine the reported gap.

For larger searches, multiple starts and one-family, two-family and
direction changes seek a better feasible plan, with a new bounded
allocation for each proposed family set. Their result remains best found
unless a global bound independently closes the gap. One call-owned C cache
shares finite-normal intensities and direct marginals across all proposals.
Its keys retain the actual counts, scoring method and numerical refinement.
The allocation work ceiling is cumulative across the complete joint call;
cached values reduce repeated integration but do not erase search work.
An unfinished allocation leaves a family set unresolved instead of
proving it infeasible. A feasible supplied incumbent remains available
when a work limit stops the search.

The common evaluator recomputes final budget, bound and practical checks
from the actual pairs, directions and counts. The printout distinguishes
baseline, response and selected mean, as well as the objective bound and
its numerical qualification. A finite-normal gap is based on estimated
quadrature and rounding uncertainty and is not an interval certificate
for the exact integrals or a validation of the finite-locus prediction.

\subsection{Two-budget allocation of produced and retained descendants}

For each allowed biological family, $y_e\in\{0,1\}$ indicates produced
activation, and $n_e,r_e$ are nonnegative integer produced and retained
counts. The constraints are
\[
 \sum_e y_e=C,\qquad\sum_e n_e=P,\qquad\sum_e r_e=R>0,
\]
\[
 L_ey_e\le n_e\le U_ey_e,\qquad
 l_ey_e\le r_e\le u_ey_e,\qquad r_e\le n_e,\qquad L_e\ge1.
\]
An inactive family has zero produced and retained counts. An active family
with $r_e=0$ still consumes production and a family slot; its retained
weight is zero. Such families require an explicit zero retained lower
bound. The example above instead has $l_e=1$ for every candidate.
The optional bounds table joins these limits by unordered parent-pair keys,
including candidates unused in the returned plan. Its specified cells
replace common scalar limits. Directions share the biological bounds.

With the intensity $a(n,r)$ defined above and common accuracy $\rho$, the
selected G1 mean and its two components are
\[
 M_{1,\mathrm{base}}=\sum_e\frac{r_e}{R}\mu_e,\qquad
 \Delta_1=\sum_{e:r_e>0}\frac{r_e}{R}\rho\sqrt{v_e}\,a(n_e,r_e),
\]
\[
 J_{G1}=M_{1,\mathrm{base}}+\Delta_1.
\]
The mean criterion uses only $M_{1,\mathrm{base}}$ in optimization.
The usefulness formula combines the earlier family prediction with actual
retained-individual weights. The finite-normal intensity is the selected
order-statistic mean of Okoye et al.\ (2026), Equations~(4)--(8); the
Burrows alternative retains its preceding definition. The two-budget
activation, weighting and constraints define the present implementation.
No intensity or selected family mean is evaluated for $r_e=0$. Its global
utility contribution is exactly zero, while full retention has
$a(n_e,n_e)=0$.

One original-score anchor $\mu_0$ permits utilities in mean units:
\[
 b_e(n,r)=\frac rR(\mu_e-\mu_0),\qquad
 d_e(n,r)=\frac rR\rho\sqrt{v_e}\,a(n,r),
 \qquad J_{G1}=\mu_0+\sum_e\{b_e+d_e\}.
\]
The C calculations retain baseline and response components separately and
form their differences before comparison. They avoid unnormalized
$r\mu_e$ intermediates and reconstruct the common anchor once, using the
integer identity $\sum_er_e=R$. This preserves the intended scale when
large shared merits coexist with a small selection response.

For fixed families and separable restrictions, the recurrence follows
the dynamic programming principle of Bellman (1954):
\[
 D_e(p,s)=\max_{(n,r)\in\mathcal A_e}
 \{D_{e-1}(p-n,s-r)+b_e(n,r)+d_e(n,r)\},
\]
where $\mathcal A_e$ is the family-specific admissible integer domain,
$D_0(0,0)=0$, and impossible states are excluded. Traceback recovers both
count vectors. The mean criterion omits $d_e$ from its comparisons.
Coupled produced or retained practical constraints need their original-input
checks; a relaxed dynamic-programming solution is not automatically feasible.


The planner's parental limits and original relatedness ceiling still use
family-slot shares $c_i^F=d_i/(2C)$. Actual retained shares instead use
\[
 c_i^R=\frac1{2R}\sum_{e:i\in e}r_e,
 \qquad q_R=(c^R)'Kc^R.
\]
The complete original reference is common to $q_R$ and the family-slot
value $q_F$. Their difference reflects nominal representation under the
declared counts; it does not describe marker-level selection within a
family. Retained-basis practical limits, equal use, parent counts and
diversity checks are recomputed with the actual $r_e$, while family-slot
restrictions retain their original incidence basis.

A bound based on the unweighted average of family scores does not bound
the variable-weight criterion. For the finite-normal calculation, a
positive-retention family upper uses
$a(U_e,\max(1,l_e))$: intensity increases with production at fixed
retention and decreases with retention at fixed production. The maximum
of such individual selected-mean uppers is a valid weak population upper
because the positive retained weights sum to one. Numerical uncertainty
remains included. A stronger retained-capacity relaxation is possible only
on distinct biological families. Burrows bounds instead require the
admissible count grid or a separately justified bound; finite-normal
monotonicity is not silently transferred to that approximation.

The search shares a call-owned count-pair cache across all family sets.
One cumulative work allowance includes tested count alternatives,
dynamic-programming transitions and unsuccessful proposals. Cached
intensities reduce repeated integration but do not remove executed search
work from that allowance. A stopped family-set allocation stays unresolved
unless a valid bound excludes it; failure to find an incumbent does not
prove infeasibility.

For small family sets, the implementation can enumerate precomputed local
utilities with the last family's counts forced by both remaining budgets.
Conservative work and workspace estimates precede that path. Other supported
separable cases use the two-budget dynamic program. Coupled constraints use
their original-input callback; an unconstrained relaxed solution remains a
bound until those checks pass. A bounded larger path alternates production
improvements at fixed retention, retention improvements at fixed production,
and two-family count blocks preserving both totals. Family and direction
exchanges use the same bounded inner solver. These searches preserve a
checked supplied incumbent but do not certify the global family-choice
problem through local improvement alone.

The variable-retention normal kernel also uses equivalent integrals in the
normal variate for counts up to 256, away from its exact boundary cases. Here
$b_{a,b}(p)$ is the beta density, $Q(z)=1-\Phi(z)$ and
$H(z)=\phi(z)-zQ(z)$. A change of variable $p=\Phi(z)$ in the preceding
beta-expectation identity gives, for $1\le r<n$,
\[
 a(n,r)=\frac nr\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
 \phi(z)^2 b_{r,n-r}\{\Phi(z)\}\,dz.
\]
The positive direct increment, for $1\le r\le n$, is equivalently
\[
 a(n+1,r)-a(n,r)=\frac1r\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
 H(z)\phi(z)b_{r,n-r+1}\{Q(z)\}\,dz.
\]
The latter follows by substituting $q=Q(z)$ in the threshold stop-loss
beta expectation. These changes of variable give equivalent representations of the normal
order-statistic objective and direct increment defined above.

The C quadrature integrates the two intervals $[-12,0]$ and $[0,12]$ using
log densities and the stable normal stop-loss calculation. For integer beta
shapes, the density is at most one less than their sum, by its binomial
probability representation. At a cutoff $T>0$, the omitted tails are bounded
respectively by
\[
 \frac{n(n-1)}r\frac{\phi(T)^2}{T},\qquad
 \frac nr\left\{\phi(T)+\frac{\phi(T)^2}{T}\right\}.
\]
For $T=12$ and $n\le256$, the implementation adds a conservative absolute
allowance of $10^{-27}$. This bounds truncation, while adaptive quadrature
and arithmetic errors remain estimated. Larger counts or an unqualified
density integration use the preceding beta-quantile calculation. The
existing fixed-retention route keeps its original integration path.

Cache entries retain count pair, method, direct-increment type, refinement
and numerical uncertainty. Successful cache hits avoid separate timing
calls; their counts remain recorded, and total inner time still includes
cache work. Small-family enumeration can reject a candidate whose coarse
outward binary64 upper is strictly below a checked incumbent's lower.
Near ties, cancellation and uncertain cases retain the full compensated
component comparison, and final winning counts are rescored. This shortcut
does not remove any charged count-search work or turn estimated normal
errors into rigorous interval certificates.

\subsection{Exact DH moments and selection-aware founder contributions}

For marker $j$, $x_{pj}\in\{0,1\}$ identifies the effect allele in
homozygous founder $p$, and $u_j$ is the complete stored dosage
coefficient. Thus the founder prediction and family contrast are
\[
 g_p=u_0+2\sum_ju_jx_{pj},\qquad
 \mu_{ab}=\frac{g_a+g_b}{2},\qquad
 d_j=u_j(x_{aj}-x_{bj}).
\]
The factor two comes from the difference between homozygous dosages zero
and two. The precise stored effects define these moments; rounded exported
parental predictions do not replace them.

$S_j=1$ denotes origin from parent $a$ and $S_j=-1$ origin from $b$.
For an immediate F1-derived DH, $E(S)=0$. Its origin correlation matrix
$\Omega$ has diagonal one, zero between chromosomes, and
\[
 \Omega_{jk}=\exp\{-2|p_j-p_k|/100\}
\]
within a chromosome when map positions $p$ are in cM. With fixed
nonnegative marker weights $w_j$ summing to one,
\[
 G=\mu_{ab}+d'S,\qquad V=d'\Omega d,\qquad
 C_a=\frac{1+w'S}{2},\qquad C_b=1-C_a,
\]
\[
 \operatorname{Var}(C_a)=\frac14w'\Omega w,\qquad
 h_{ab}=\operatorname{Cov}(C_a,G)=\frac12w'\Omega d.
\]
These unselected identities are exact under the declared map and fixed
additive effects. The covariance contractions specialize Allier et al.\
(2019a), Equations~(2) and~(4a,b), and Allier et al.\ (2019b),
Equations~(4b) and~(5). The DH origin correlation follows the preceding
Osthushenrich et al.\ (2017) formulas. Origins remain labelled where the
observed parental alleles coincide.

The implementation privately sorts markers within chromosomes. Exponential
forward and backward recurrences calculate $\Omega w$ and the origin
variance in linear time. Prepared founder sums
$T_p=\sum_j(\Omega w)_ju_jx_{pj}$ give
$h_{ab}=(T_a-T_b)/2$; a compensated direct contrast sum is used when that
subtraction loses material precision. The trait variance uses a positive
linear-time recurrence. Within a chromosome, with adjacent decay
$q_j=\exp(-2\Delta_j)$ in Morgans,
\[
 F_1=d_1,\qquad F_j=d_j+q_jF_{j-1},\qquad
 V_{\mathrm{chr}}=F_m^2+\sum_{j<m}(1-q_{j+1}^2)F_j^2.
\]
Innovation weights use \cde{expm1} to preserve small distances. Chromosome
resets and equal positions retain the same origin law. A fixed founder
anchor and direct contrasts protect genetic deviations and covariances
from a large common intercept. Family moments are cached independently
of produced counts, retained counts and the selection method.

For positive variance, the declared ranking score is
\[
 I=\rho\frac{G-\mu}{\sqrt V}+\sqrt{1-\rho^2}\,\varepsilon,
 \qquad \varepsilon\sim N(0,1),
\]
with independent noise. A joint-normal regression approximation gives
\[
 c_{a,e}^*=\frac12+\rho\,a(n_e,r_e)\frac{h_e}{\sqrt{V_e}},
 \qquad c_{b,e}^*=1-c_{a,e}^*,\quad r_e>0.
\]
The contribution regression follows Allier et al.\ (2019a),
Equations~(5) and~(6a--c), and Allier et al.\ (2019b), Equation~(7).
The latter is written for accuracy one. Its use with the declared
independent-noise accuracy factor and the finite-family intensity
$a(n,r)$ is the present adaptation. The normal intensity is the
order-statistic mean cited from Okoye et al.\ (2026), Equations~(4)--(8);
the Burrows option keeps its preceding definition. The approximation
concerns the conditional distribution of bounded origin variables, even
when the normal integral is evaluated accurately.

Full retention, accuracy zero or constant genetic values give exactly
one-half expected parental origin under neutral ranking. A zero retained
count has no conditional selected summary and contributes zero population
weight. Finite raw estimates outside $[0,1]$ remain visible. Their qualified
contribution and proxy are unavailable with a stated approximation reason;
values within that interval can still be biased.

\subsection{Founder proxy, constraints and actual genomic metrics}

With $R=\sum_er_e>0$, the analytic aggregate contribution is
\[
 c_p^*=\sum_{e:r_e>0}\frac{r_e}{R}
 \{\mathbf1(p=a_e)c_{a,e}^*+\mathbf1(p=b_e)c_{b,e}^*\},
 \qquad Q_{\mathrm{proxy}}=(c^*)'Kc^*.
\]
This is the separately named \cde{ucpc.founder.proxy} on the complete
original supplied reference. Unused reference parents have zero
contribution. Allier et al.\ (2019b), Equations~(8)--(10), motivate the
selected-population objective and diversity constraint; the current
retained-count weighting and supplied-$K$ convention are explicit here.
An arbitrary supplied genomic matrix does not make $1-Q_{\mathrm{proxy}}$
a gene-diversity value.

For a finite realized origin vector $c_{\mathrm{real}}$, the quadratic
expectation identity is
\[
 E(c_{\mathrm{real}}'Kc_{\mathrm{real}})
 =E(c_{\mathrm{real}})'KE(c_{\mathrm{real}})
  +\operatorname{tr}\{K\operatorname{Var}(c_{\mathrm{real}})\}.
\]
The mean-contribution proxy omits the covariance term. It also averages
origins over markers before using the supplied founder matrix. The
discussion ``Predicting the Next-Generation Diversity'' in Allier et al.\
(2019b) identifies the consequences of replacing locus-specific ancestry
by genome-average contributions.

Without \cde{selected.founder.theta}, these predictions are report-only
and leave the optimization problem unchanged. An active cap imposes
$Q_{\mathrm{proxy}}\le\theta_{\mathrm{selected}}$ in addition to the
original family-slot and practical restrictions. It couples the actual
produced and retained counts. Qualified analytic passes accept a candidate;
an unavailable approximation or unresolved comparison cannot prove
infeasibility. The same actual-count callback checks fixed-retention and
variable-retention solves. Numerical intensity errors propagate through
the contribution and quadratic calculations, with estimated arithmetic
uncertainty. They do not bound the regression approximation bias.
Fractional contributions do not become literal PropSD multiplicities.

For the scenario allele metric, source reference dosages give fixed
frequencies $p_{0j}$ and $D=\sum_jw_jp_{0j}(1-p_{0j})$. With
$X=Z-2p_0$, the weighted construction is
\[
 A_w=\frac{X\operatorname{diag}(w)X'}{2D},\qquad
 K_{\mathrm{genomic}}=A_w/2.
\]
This is the weighted extension of the first method in VanRaden (2008,
p.~4416; unnumbered formula). For one retained DH panel with allele
frequencies $p_{sj}$, the all-pairs mean including diagonals is
\[
 Q_s=\frac{\sum_jw_j(p_{sj}-p_{0j})^2}{D},\qquad
 H_s=2\sum_jw_jp_{sj}(1-p_{sj}).
\]
The metric name is
\cde{weighted.\allowbreak vanraden.\allowbreak A.\allowbreak over.\allowbreak 2.\allowbreak fixed.\allowbreak reference}.
It uses the fixed source reference and sampling with replacement over
retained pairs; it is not re-centered on the selected lines. A zero
reference denominator leaves $Q_s$ unavailable. Population gene diversity
$H_s$ remains distinct from individual DH heterozygosity, which is zero.
Nonlinear $Q_s$ and $H_s$ are calculated inside each replicate and then
averaged, preserving their finite-panel estimands.

\subsection{Actual-genotype scenarios, variance and inheritance}

Each chromosome starts at either founder with equal probability and
switches parental origin over adjacent distance $\Delta$ Morgans with
probability $(1-\exp(-2\Delta))/2$. The scenario algorithm streams
candidate genetic deviations and rank priorities into a top-$r$ heap,
then regenerates the selected origin paths. It uses theoretical family
variance in the ranking law, with neutral independent priorities for
ties. Actual origin labels remain available at identical alleles.
Genotypes are generated only for the requested final assessment.

The private bank records its protocol, core model, seed and replicate.
Primitive candidate keys use the unordered biological family, candidate,
chromosome and separate origin, noise and tie domains. Produced and
retained counts, accuracy and display order are excluded from those
primitive keys. Larger produced counts reuse candidate prefixes, and
changed retention or accuracy reuses the same genetic paths and priorities.
Streaming regeneration avoids a full produced-genotype tensor. Caller
RNGs, population identities and marker-data state remain independent of
this private assessment.

For replicate $s$ with $R$ selected genetic values, $\overline G_s$
is their mean and
\[
 V_s=\frac1R\sum_{i=1}^R(G_{si}-\overline G_s)^2.
\]
The expected finite-panel variance is $E(V_s)$. Expected empirical sample
variance uses $R V_s/(R-1)$ for $R>1$. The variance of a randomly chosen
selected individual is
\[
 V_{\mathrm{marginal}}=E(V_s)+\operatorname{Var}(\overline G_s).
\]
Its estimate combines the mean panel variance with the unbiased sample
variance of replicate panel means, requiring multiple replicates. At
$R=1$, panel variance is zero, empirical sample variance is undefined,
and marginal variance may remain positive. The same distinctions apply
within each family. Centered accumulation preserves small genetic
variation in the presence of a large shared intercept.

Ordinary metric SE uses the sample standard deviation of independent
whole-replicate values divided by the square root of the completed
replicate count. One replicate has no such SE. The marginal-variance SE
is explicitly unavailable in the present implementation. A work-limited
bank reports completed whole replicates and its partial status; no
partial current replicate contributes to the published estimates.
Analytic proxy feasibility and scenario completion retain separate states.

The normalized model stores an immutable versioned snapshot of candidate
alleles, effects, map, weights and fixed reference information. Canonical
linkage-group keys use the smallest marker identifier in each group.
Evaluation and allocation inherit that core and its original cap; live
dataset changes do not rebind it. Current actual counts and the current
selection method determine a new analytic assessment. Scenario execution
requires an explicit current request, so inherited historical scenario
results do not become predictions for changed counts. This model describes
the selected G1 DH panel; a further crossing and DH cycle requires the
separate future-population policy.

\subsection{Actual selected DH lines and a second crossing cycle}

The current design $p$ contains the active founder pairs and their produced
and retained counts $(n_{1e},r_{1e})$. Its fixed totals are $C_1$, $P_1$
and $R_1=\sum_e r_{1e}$. Actual F1-derived DH candidates follow the
preceding additive Haldane model and the declared independent-noise index.
The selected logical lines retain their original family and candidate
identities, including distinct lines with identical observed genotypes.

For even $R_1\geq2$, a uniform permutation of all selected lines is split
into adjacent pairs. This gives a uniform disjoint matching and uses every
line once. The next cycle has $C_2=R_1/2$ pairs, with uniform produced count
$n_2$, retained count $r_2$ and accuracy $\rho_2$; hence
$P_2=C_2n_2$ and $R_2=C_2r_2$. The policy permits two distinct selected
lines from the same original family. It does not choose the future mates
adaptively or reuse a selected parent.

For actual homozygous G1 allele vectors $x$ and $y$, the new F1 has
\[
 \mu_{xy}=\frac{G(x)+G(y)}2,\qquad
 d_j=u_j(x_j-y_j),\qquad V_{xy}=d'\Omega d.
\]
Here $u_j$, $G$ and $\Omega$ have the preceding dosage and Haldane
definitions. A new G2 DH has genetic value $\mu_{xy}+d'S$ from an actual
origin path $S$. The already homozygous DH parents themselves have zero
segregation; variation arises after their new cross. Conditional variance
uses the positive linear-time recurrence stated above, not a kinship or
mean-ancestry replacement. The reported pair diagnostics
$E(V_{xy})$ and $E(\sqrt{V_{xy}})$ are distinct. In general,
$E(\sqrt{V_{xy}})\ne\sqrt{E(V_{xy})}$, and neither diagnostic replaces
the actual two-cycle calculation.

The target is the expected selected G2 mean under this declared policy,
\[
 J_2(p)=E_p\!\left(\overline G_{2,\mathrm{selected}}\right).
\]
Each realized matching has average unselected G2 family mean equal to
its selected G1 mean: every G1 line appears once and every G2 pair has
the same retained weight. With
\[
 B_1(p)=\frac{\sum_e r_{1e}\mu_e}{R_1},\qquad
 \Delta_{1s}=\overline G_{1s}-B_1(p),\qquad
 \Delta_{2s}=\overline G_{2s}-\overline G_{1s},
\]
the component identity is
\[
 J_2(p)=B_1(p)+E_p(\Delta_1)+E_p(\Delta_2).
\]
The two responses in this identity arise from actual sampled genotypes.
The earlier normal-model G1 usefulness and its analytic response retain
their separate definitions. Neutral G2 selection or full retention gives
$E_p(\Delta_2)=0$; a finite realized bank can have a nonzero response.

Niehoff et al.\ (2024), Equation~(2), decomposes gametic variance among
selected offspring. Their Equation~(8) is an approximate two-generation
selection criterion; it is not the actual matched-DH calculation above.
Equation~(9) and Appendix~1 give mean offspring gametic sampling variance
as $(\sigma_{DH}^2-\sigma_{FS}^2)/4$. The factor four converts their
diploid-DH variance to a gametic scale. The directly computed $V_{xy}$
above already describes diploid DH values and is not divided by four.
The actual mating and two DH generations define the present adaptation.
Future-oriented progeny allocation is also the topic of Hamazaki and
Iwata (2024), Sections~1 and~2.1.

\subsection{Finite-bank search and paired future assessment}

A fixed training bank with $S_T$ complete replicates defines
\[
 \widehat J_{2,T}(p)=\frac1{S_T}\sum_{s=1}^{S_T}
                          \overline G_{2s}(p).
\]
Every competing candidate uses this same complete bank. An interrupted
partial training bank supplies no comparable candidate score. Existing
G1 solvers supply checked seeds and feasibility repairs; their separable
G1 bounds are not bounds for $J_2$. The joint search preserves current
production and retention totals through count transfers, joint count
blocks and family exchanges, subject to the actual current constraints.
A best-found status describes the finite training search. Exhaustive
small-domain completion can establish an optimum of that bank, while
the gap to the unknown expectation optimum remains unavailable without
a separate valid bound.

Held-out evaluation uses a separate role domain. For two actual designs
$p$ and $q$, the common evaluation bank gives paired differences
\[
 D_s=\overline G_{2s}(p)-\overline G_{2s}(q),\qquad
 \widehat D=\frac1{S_E}\sum_{s=1}^{S_E}D_s,
\]
\[
 \operatorname{MCSE}(\widehat D)=
 \sqrt{\frac{\sum_s(D_s-\widehat D)^2}{S_E(S_E-1)}},
 \qquad S_E>1.
\]
The same construction applies to G1 means and the additional G2
response. Adding unrelated design-level SEs does not preserve this
pairing. Held-out observations do not select a training incumbent,
stopping rule or later frontier warm start.

The private two-cycle bank separates core, role, stage, seed and replicate.
G1 primitive keys use intrinsic original-family and candidate identities.
Selected identities are sorted before the descending Fisher--Yates
permutation of Durstenfeld (1964), Algorithm~235. Unbiased integer
rejection supplies each permutation choice from the private bank.
Realized unordered pairs are oriented by logical identity
and sorted into matching slots. G2 path, index-noise and tie primitives
use slot and candidate keys, with independent slots within a replicate.
The same actual pair can occupy another slot in another design, so a
cached pair response also depends on slot and path identity. This common
bank preserves each design's marginal law without claiming identical
trajectories for every pair across designs. It is separate from the
preceding single-cycle scenario protocol.

Signed trait sums use compensated accumulation following Neumaier
(1974). For an accumulated main term $s$, residual $c$ and new summand
$a$, the update has the form
\[
 t=s+a,\qquad s'=t,\qquad
 c'=c+\begin{cases}
       (s-t)+a,& |s|\geq|a|,\\
       (a-t)+s,& |s|<|a|,
      \end{cases}
\]
with the final sum represented by $s+c$. The implementation applies
this protection to signed anchor, family-contrast and future-trait
boundaries. Centered components preserve small responses beside a large
common mean; scale-aware variance accumulation protects the corresponding
second moments. These safeguards do not provide arbitrary precision.
An unrepresentable required quantity has an explicit numerical-range
status.

Bounded reusable caches retain intrinsic candidate paths, selected
mosaics, pair moments and slot-specific results. One cumulative cell and
work budget covers all search candidates, frontier points and final
assessment. Reserved final-assessment work remains inside that budget.
Incomplete final banks report only jointly completed whole replicates
and their completion status. Resource limits do not establish biological
infeasibility, and a partial result does not become a full-bank certificate.

Actual G1 and G2 genomic metrics use their realized finite panels, the
unchanged fixed reference and the preceding $Q_s$, $H_s$ and variance
definitions. Nonlinear metrics are averaged after calculation inside
each replicate. Monte Carlo SE describes simulation precision under
fixed fitted effects. The accuracy and shrinkage qualifications in
Niehoff et al.\ (2025), Sections~4.1 and~4.4, remain relevant; this
assessment adds no phenotypic information or effect-estimation uncertainty.

\subsection{Labelled gain--diversity frontier}

Each declared frontier limit replaces one named current G1 constraint:
family-slot $\theta$ or the qualified selected-founder UCPC proxy cap.
Other constraints and total resources remain fixed. Default independent
starts retain the recorded standalone start and seed at every point;
optional warm starts carry actual previous counts and pairs with an
accepted or fallback status. Core data and private banks can be shared,
while the work budget covers the whole frontier call.

Every point retains its actual search, feasibility and assessment status.
A complete declared schedule agrees with its corresponding standalone
problem under the same recorded inputs and sufficient matching resources.
A capped schedule remains explicitly incomplete. The horizontal metric
describes a G1 constraint, whereas actual G2 genomic $Q$ and $H$ are
additional estimates. The proxy cap is not a hard bound on actual G2
genomic coancestry. Sample-based or bounded-search points need not be
monotone and do not establish a smooth global tradeoff curve.

\section{Cross selection from external diploid inputs}
\label{app:external-diploid-crosses}

We now select five crosses from externally supplied marker data and
marker effects. The examples use the first ten fully homozygous diploid
parents and first 100 markers for additive yield, a two-trait additive
index, and the same index with dominance. The input file
\cde{crossing-example-inputs.rds} is prepared separately and located in
the working directory; no input data are included.

The RDS object is a list with \cde{markers}, \cde{additive},
\cde{dominance}, and \cde{weights}. The numeric marker matrix has parents
in rows, markers in columns, unique row and column names, and at least
ten rows and 100 columns. Dosages are 0 or 2 with no missing values.
The additive and dominance components are named lists with \cde{YLD}
and \cde{MAT} vectors named by marker. The numeric weights are named
\cde{YLD} and \cde{MAT}. Effects and weights are finite and use compatible
trait units and allele orientation; larger index values are preferred.

\subsection{Family means and variances}

All 45 unordered nonself crosses are evaluated with an independent-marker
approximation that omits linkage covariance. For dosage $z$, a marker
contributes $az+d\,1_{\{z=1\}}$, with dominance expressed as the
heterozygote deviation from the additive midpoint and no fitted
intercept. Three selfing cycles after $F_1$ give $F_4$ progeny and
heterozygosity $h=1/8$ at loci where the parents differ. At these loci,
the mean dominance contribution is $hd$ and the variance is
$(1-h)a^2+h(1-h)d^2$. Equal parental loci have no segregation variance.
Trait effects are weighted before the index moments are calculated,
retaining covariance induced by effects at the same marker.

The expected superior progeny score is $\mu+i\sigma$, where
$i=\phi(\Phi^{-1}(1-5/10))/(5/10)$. This normal selection approximation
represents retaining five of ten progeny, without a finite-population
intensity correction.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
input <- readRDS("crossing-example-inputs.rds")
cross.example.prepare <- function(input) {
    X <- input$markers
    stopifnot(is.matrix(X), is.numeric(X), nrow(X) >= 10,
              ncol(X) >= 100)
    X <- X[seq_len(10), seq_len(100), drop = FALSE]
    stopifnot(!anyNA(X), all(X %in% c(0, 2)),
              !is.null(rownames(X)), !is.null(colnames(X)),
              !anyDuplicated(rownames(X)), !anyDuplicated(colnames(X)))
    loci <- colnames(X)
    effects <- function(x) {
        stopifnot(is.numeric(x), !is.null(names(x)),
                  !anyDuplicated(names(x)), all(loci %in% names(x)))
        out <- unname(x[loci])
        stopifnot(all(is.finite(out)))
        out
    }
    a.yield <- effects(input$additive$YLD)
    stopifnot(is.numeric(input$weights),
              !anyDuplicated(names(input$weights)),
              all(c("YLD", "MAT") %in% names(input$weights)))
    weights <- input$weights[c("YLD", "MAT")]
    stopifnot(all(is.finite(weights)))
    a.index <- weights[1] * a.yield +
               weights[2] * effects(input$additive$MAT)
    d.index <- weights[1] * effects(input$dominance$YLD) +
               weights[2] * effects(input$dominance$MAT)
    centered <- sweep(X, 2, colMeans(X), "-")
    p <- colMeans(X) / 2
    scale <- 2 * sum(p * (1 - p))
    stopifnot(is.finite(scale), scale > 0)
    G <- tcrossprod(centered) / scale
    dimnames(G) <- list(rownames(X), rownames(X))
    pairs <- t(combn(rownames(X), 2))
    h <- 2^(-3)
    intensity <- dnorm(qnorm(1 - 5 / 10)) / (5 / 10)
    score <- function(a, d = rep(0, ncol(X))) {
        mu <- va <- numeric(nrow(pairs))
        for (j in seq_len(nrow(pairs))) {
            x1 <- X[pairs[j, 1], ]
            x2 <- X[pairs[j, 2], ]
            segregating <- x1 != x2
            mu[j] <- sum((x1 + x2) * a / 2) +
                     h * sum(d[segregating])
            va[j] <- sum(((1 - h) * a^2 +
                          h * (1 - h) * d^2)[segregating])
        }
        data.frame(P1Name = pairs[, 1], P2Name = pairs[, 2],
                   mu = mu, va = va,
                   es = mu + sqrt(va) * intensity)
    }
    list(markers = X, G = G, K = G / 2,
         effects = list(yield = a.yield, index = a.index,
                        dominance = a.index),
         scores = list(yield = score(a.yield), index = score(a.index),
                       dominance = score(a.index, d.index)),
         accuracy = 0.5, loss.limit = 0.05,
         n.crosses = 5L, n.progeny = 10L, n.selected = 5L)
}
example <- cross.example.prepare(input)
@

\subsection{Three crossing plans}

Each plan contains five distinct nonself crosses, ten progeny per
family and five retained per family: 50 produced and 25 retained. The
planner selects the families with these fixed counts. Its accuracy
of 0.5 adjusts the supplied within-family selection differential once,
giving $\mu+0.5i\sigma$.

Allele frequencies from the ten-parent panel define the marker
relationship matrix and \cde{K=G/2}. The relatedness bound and maximum
contribution of 0.5 are nonrestrictive here. The diversity constraint
requires PropSD strictly below 0.05. Its sample comprises the ten
parental occurrences in the selected families, including repeated
occurrences of a parent used in several families. The full ten-parent
matrix remains the reference. This covariance-based expected SD loss
differs from realized variation among selected descendants.
Appendix~\ref{app:cross-mathematics} gives its definition.

The first plan uses additive yield effects.
<<eval=FALSE>>=
cross.example.plan <- function(example, scenario) {
    merit <- setNames(drop(example$markers %*% example$effects[[scenario]]),
                      rownames(example$markers))
    SelectionTools::st.cross.plan(
        merit = merit, K = example$K,
        n.crosses = example$n.crosses, n.progeny = example$n.progeny,
        theta = max(diag(example$K)), upper = 0.5,
        scores = example$scores[[scenario]],
        criterion = "usefulness", accuracy = example$accuracy,
        diversity = list(G = example$G, max = example$loss.limit,
                         sample = "family.slots"),
        control = list(method = "exact", max.nodes = 3000000,
                       starts = 4, maxit = 2500, seed = 1L)
    )
}
yield.plan <- cross.example.plan(example, "yield")
yield.plan
@
